window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-chem-biomolecules","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Biomolecules · Practice","chapter":"Biomolecules","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (vitamin) with List-II (deficiency disease):\nList-I\n(A) Vitamin B1\n(B) Vitamin B2\n(C) Vitamin B6\n(D) Vitamin B12\nList-II\n(I) Convulsions\n(II) Pernicious anaemia\n(III) Beri beri\n(IV) Cheilosis","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"B1 (thiamine) deficiency causes beri beri, so A matches III.\nB2 (riboflavin) deficiency causes cheilosis, which is fissuring at the corners of the mouth and lips. So B matches IV.\nB6 (pyridoxine) deficiency causes convulsions, so C matches I.\nB12 deficiency causes pernicious anaemia, where RBCs are deficient in haemoglobin. So D matches II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Amylose is the water-soluble component of starch. It makes up about 15–20% of starch and is an unbranched chain of $\\alpha$-D-glucose units joined by C1–C4 glycosidic linkages.\nStatement II: Glycogen resembles amylopectin in structure but is less branched than amylopectin.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Amylose is water soluble, forms 15–20% of starch and is a long unbranched chain of 200–1000 $\\alpha$-D-glucose units linked C1–C4.\nStatement II is incorrect. Glycogen, called animal starch, is structurally similar to amylopectin but is MORE highly branched, not less.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) $\\alpha$-D-glucose and $\\beta$-D-glucose differ only in the configuration of the –OH group at C1.\n(b) The $\\alpha$-form of glucose (m.p. 419 K) is obtained by crystallisation from a concentrated solution at 303 K.\n(c) Glucose pentaacetate reacts with hydroxylamine to form an oxime.\n(d) In its cyclic forms, fructose forms a six-membered pyranose ring through the –OH at C5.\n(e) The D/L configuration of glucose is assigned by comparing the –OH on its lowest asymmetric carbon with glyceraldehyde.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: The anomers differ only at C1, the anomeric carbon.\n(b) Correct: The $\\alpha$-form (419 K) crystallises at 303 K, while the $\\beta$-form (423 K) crystallises at 371 K.\n(c) Incorrect: The pentaacetate does NOT react with hydroxylamine, which shows there is no free –CHO group.\n(d) Incorrect: The C5 –OH of fructose forms a five-membered furanose ring.\n(e) Correct: Only the lowest asymmetric carbon is compared with glyceraldehyde.\nCorrect statements = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the preparation of glucose.\nStatement I: When sucrose is boiled with dilute HCl in alcoholic solution, it gives glucose and fructose in equal amounts.\nStatement II: Commercially, glucose is obtained by boiling starch with dilute $H_2SO_4$ at 393 K under a pressure of 2-3 atm.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Acid hydrolysis of sucrose gives one molecule of glucose and one molecule of fructose: $C_{12}H_{22}O_{11} + H_2O \\rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6 + C_6H_{12}O_6$. So the two sugars form in equal amounts.\nStatement II is correct. Starch, $(C_6H_{10}O_5)_n$, is hydrolysed commercially by dilute $H_2SO_4$ at 393 K and 2-3 atm to give $nC_6H_{12}O_6$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following amino acids are essential amino acids, according to Table 10.2 of NCERT?\nValine, Glycine, Lysine, Serine, Threonine, Phenylalanine, Tyrosine, Histidine, Alanine","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Essential amino acids cannot be synthesised by the body and must come from the diet. Table 10.2 marks ten of them: Val, Leu, Ile, Arg, Lys, Thr, Met, Phe, Trp and His.\nEssential ones in the given list: valine, lysine, threonine, phenylalanine and histidine, which makes 5.\nNon-essential ones in the list: glycine, serine, tyrosine and alanine. Tyrosine is the tempting trap, because it is structurally close to phenylalanine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"When an egg is boiled, the egg white coagulates because its proteins are denatured. Which level of protein structure remains intact after denaturation?","image":"","options":["Primary structure","Secondary structure","Tertiary structure","Both secondary and tertiary structures"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Denaturation is caused by heat or a change in pH. These disturb the hydrogen bonds, so the globules unfold and the helices uncoil.\nAs a result, the secondary and tertiary structures are destroyed.\nThe peptide bonds are not broken, so the amino acid sequence (primary structure) stays intact.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following compounds:\nLactic acid $CH_3CH(OH)COOH$, glyceraldehyde $CH_2(OH)CH(OH)CHO$, formaldehyde $HCHO$, acetic acid $CH_3COOH$, rhamnose $C_6H_{12}O_5$, sucrose $C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}$, glucose $C_6H_{12}O_6$.\nHow many of these BOTH fit the formula $C_x(H_2O)_y$ AND are carbohydrates by the chemical definition?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Lactic acid is $C_3(H_2O)_3$, so it fits. It is a hydroxy acid, not a polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone, so it is not a carbohydrate.\nGlyceraldehyde is $C_3(H_2O)_3$, so it fits. It is an optically active polyhydroxy aldehyde, so it is a carbohydrate. ✔\nFormaldehyde is $C(H_2O)$, so it fits. It is neither polyhydroxy nor optically active, so it is not a carbohydrate.\nAcetic acid fits but is not a carbohydrate.\nRhamnose is a carbohydrate but does not fit the formula.\nSucrose is $C_{12}(H_2O)_{11}$ and gives monosaccharides on hydrolysis. ✔\nGlucose is $C_6(H_2O)_6$ and is a carbohydrate. ✔\nTotal = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about carbohydrates is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Polysaccharides are called non-sugars because they are not sweet in taste","Monosaccharides can be hydrolysed to simpler polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone units","The term saccharide comes from the Greek word sakcharon, meaning sugar","On hydrolysis, maltose gives only glucose whereas sucrose gives glucose and fructose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: polysaccharides such as starch and cellulose are not sweet, hence the name non-sugars.\n(2) Incorrect: by definition, a monosaccharide cannot be hydrolysed further into simpler polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone units.\n(3) Correct: 'saccharide' comes from the Greek sakcharon, meaning sugar.\n(4) Correct: maltose gives 2 glucose molecules, while sucrose gives glucose and fructose.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A monomer unit isolated from a nucleic acid has the following features: its pentose sugar carries an H atom instead of an –OH group at C-2′, and the nitrogenous base attached at C-1′ forms hydrogen bonds with adenine in the complementary strand. The monomer is","image":"","options":["a thymine-containing nucleotide of DNA","a uracil-containing nucleotide of RNA","a cytosine-containing nucleotide of DNA","a thymine-containing nucleotide of RNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: A sugar with no –OH at C-2′ is 2-deoxyribose. This sugar occurs only in DNA, so the monomer comes from DNA.\nStep 2: In DNA, adenine pairs with thymine and cytosine pairs with guanine. The base that pairs with adenine is therefore thymine.\nStep 3: Uracil is found only in RNA, and RNA contains ribose, which has a 2′-OH. Thymine does not occur in RNA, so options 2 and 4 are wrong. Cytosine pairs with guanine, so option 3 is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Consider a disaccharide X made of two α-D-glucose units, in which C1 of one glucose unit is linked through oxygen to C1 of the other glucose unit. Using the NCERT criterion for reducing and non-reducing disaccharides, which prediction about X is correct?","image":"","options":["X is reducing, like maltose, because it contains only glucose units","X is non-reducing, because the anomeric (aldehydic) carbons of both units are involved in the glycosidic bond","X is reducing, because each glucose unit still has a free primary –CH2OH group","X is non-reducing, because it contains α-linked rather than β-linked units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"By the NCERT criterion, a disaccharide is non-reducing if the reducing groups (aldehydic or ketonic carbons) of both units are bonded.\nIn X, C1 of both glucose units forms the link, so no hemiacetal can open to give a free –CHO. X is therefore non-reducing.\nMaltose is reducing only because one C1 stays free; containing glucose alone does not make a sugar reducing.\nThe α or β type of the link does not decide reducing character. Maltose is α-linked and still reducing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"An aldohexose is drawn in the standard Fischer arrangement with –CHO at the top. Its –OH groups are on the left at C-2, right at C-3, left at C-4 and left at C-5. Given that natural glucose is D-(+)-glucose, this compound is:","image":"","options":["D-(+)-glucose","L-(+)-glucose","D-(–)-glucose","L-(–)-glucose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: in D-(+)-glucose the –OH groups are right (C-2), left (C-3), right (C-4) and right (C-5).\nStep 2: the given compound has every one of these reversed, so it is the mirror image (enantiomer) of D-glucose.\nStep 3: the C-5 –OH (lowest asymmetric carbon) is on the left, so the compound is in the L-series.\nStep 4: enantiomers rotate plane-polarised light by equal amounts in opposite directions. Since D-glucose is (+), this compound is (–).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"The activation energy for the acid hydrolysis of sucrose is 6.22 kJ $mol^{-1}$. When sucrose is hydrolysed by the enzyme sucrase, it is 2.15 kJ $mol^{-1}$. By what percentage does sucrase lower the activation energy relative to acid hydrolysis?","image":"","options":["65.4 %","34.6 %","189.3 %","26.5 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E_a$(acid) = 6.22 kJ/mol and $E_a$(sucrase) = 2.15 kJ/mol.\nFormula: % lowering = $\\frac{E_{a,acid} - E_{a,enz}}{E_{a,acid}} \\times 100$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{6.22 - 2.15}{6.22} \\times 100 = \\frac{4.07}{6.22} \\times 100$\nSo the lowering is about 65.4 %.\nOption (2), 34.6 %, is the fraction of the barrier that remains, not the fraction removed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"The D or L configuration of a monosaccharide such as glucose is assigned by comparing it with glyceraldehyde. Which part of the glucose molecule is used for this comparison?","image":"","options":["The –OH group on C-2, the carbon next to the –CHO group","The –OH group on the lowest asymmetric carbon, i.e. the chiral carbon farthest from the –CHO group","The direction in which the compound rotates plane-polarised light","The –CH$_2$OH group at C-6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: the most oxidised carbon (–CHO) is written at the top. Only the lowest asymmetric carbon is compared with glyceraldehyde.\nIn (+)-glucose, the –OH on C-5 lies on the right. This matches D-(+)-glyceraldehyde, so glucose is D.\nC-2 fails because the other asymmetric carbons are ignored in this comparison.\nOptical rotation fails because D/L has no relation with the sign of rotation.\nC-6 (–CH$_2$OH) fails because it is not asymmetric.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs consists only of globular proteins?","image":"","options":["Keratin and myosin","Insulin and albumin","Keratin and insulin","Myosin and albumin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Globular proteins form when polypeptide chains coil into a spherical shape. They are usually water-soluble, for example insulin and albumins.\nFibrous proteins have parallel chains held by hydrogen and disulphide bonds. They are generally water-insoluble, for example keratin (hair, wool, silk) and myosin (muscles).\nOptions (1), (3) and (4) each contain at least one fibrous protein.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Water-soluble vitamins must normally be supplied regularly in the diet because they are excreted in urine. Which one of the following is the exception that can be stored in the body?","image":"","options":["Vitamin C","Vitamin $B_1$","Vitamin $B_{12}$","Vitamin $B_6$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT groups the B-complex vitamins and vitamin C as water soluble.\nThese vitamins are readily excreted in urine and are not stored, with one exception: vitamin $B_{12}$.\nVitamin C is the most tempting wrong option. However, it is water soluble and cannot be stored, which is why it must be taken daily.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hormone) with List-II (function).\nList-I:\n(a) Glucocorticoids\n(b) Mineralocorticoids\n(c) Progesterone\n(d) Estradiol\nList-II:\n(i) Prepares the uterus for implantation of the fertilised egg\n(ii) Controls carbohydrate metabolism and modulates inflammatory reactions\n(iii) Participates in control of the menstrual cycle\n(iv) Controls excretion of water and salt by the kidney","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Glucocorticoids control carbohydrate metabolism, modulate inflammatory reactions and are involved in reactions to stress: (ii).\n(b) Mineralocorticoids control the excretion of water and salt by the kidney: (iv).\n(c) Progesterone prepares the uterus for implantation of the fertilised egg: (i).\n(d) Estradiol, the main female sex hormone, takes part in control of the menstrual cycle: (iii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"The cyclic structure of D-fructose is formed when an –OH group adds to its keto group. Which option correctly describes this ring?","image":"","options":["–OH at C-5 adds to the C-1 carbonyl, forming a six-membered pyranose ring","–OH at C-6 adds to the C-2 carbonyl, forming a six-membered pyranose ring","–OH at C-5 adds to the C-2 carbonyl, forming a five-membered furanose ring","–OH at C-4 adds to the C-2 carbonyl, forming a five-membered furanose ring"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fructose is a ketohexose. Its keto group is at C-2.\nThe –OH on C-5 adds to this C-2 carbonyl. The ring then holds C-2, C-3, C-4, C-5 and one oxygen, so it is five-membered.\nThis ring is called furanose, by analogy with furan (one O and four C in a five-membered ring).\nThe six-membered pyranose ring belongs to glucose, so options 1 and 2 are wrong. Option 4 uses the wrong –OH.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) RNA contains $\\beta$-D-ribose as its sugar.\n(ii) DNA is a single-stranded helix that sometimes folds back on itself.\n(iii) RNA molecules are of three types: mRNA, rRNA and tRNA.\n(iv) Proteins are synthesised by RNA molecules, but the message for a particular protein is present in DNA.\n(v) Har Gobind Khorana shared the 1968 Nobel Prize for cracking the genetic code.\n(vi) Watson, Crick and Wilkins proposed that DNA is a single helix like a twisted rope.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the sugar in RNA is $\\beta$-D-ribose.\n(ii) Incorrect: a single helix that sometimes folds back describes RNA. DNA is a double helix.\n(iii) Correct: the three types are mRNA, rRNA and tRNA.\n(iv) Correct: RNA molecules make proteins, but DNA carries the message.\n(v) Correct: Khorana shared the 1968 prize with Nirenberg and Holley.\n(vi) Incorrect: they proposed a double helix, like a twisted ladder.\nCorrect statements: 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Every compound having the formula $C_x(H_2O)_y$ is a carbohydrate.\n(ii) Rhamnose is a carbohydrate even though it does not fit the formula $C_x(H_2O)_y$.\n(iii) All monosaccharides, including ketoses such as fructose, reduce Fehling's solution and Tollens' reagent.\n(iv) Sucrose is a reducing sugar because its hydrolysis gives two reducing monosaccharides.\n(v) Polysaccharides are called non-sugars because they are not sweet in taste.\n(vi) An aldohexose and a ketohexose have the same molecular formula.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: acetic acid, $C_2(H_2O)_2$, fits the formula but is not a carbohydrate.\n(ii) Correct: rhamnose, $C_6H_{12}O_5$, is a carbohydrate that does not fit the formula.\n(iii) Correct: all monosaccharides, aldose or ketose, are reducing sugars.\n(iv) Incorrect: in sucrose the reducing groups (glucose C1, fructose C2) are bonded together, so sucrose itself is non-reducing. Its hydrolysis products being reducing does not change this.\n(v) Correct: this is why polysaccharides are called non-sugars.\n(vi) Correct: glucose and fructose are both $C_6H_{12}O_6$.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In D-(+)-glucose, the letter 'D' means that the –OH group on the lowest asymmetric carbon (C-5) lies on the right side when the structure is written with –CHO at the top.\nStatement II: In D-(+)-glucose, the letter 'D' shows that glucose rotates plane-polarised light to the right.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. 'D' is assigned by comparing the lowest asymmetric carbon with D-(+)-glyceraldehyde, where the –OH group is on the right. The most oxidised carbon (–CHO) is written at the top.\nStatement II is incorrect. Dextrorotation is shown by '(+)'. NCERT stresses that 'D' and 'L' have no relation to optical activity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Glycogen is also known as animal starch.\nReason (R): Glycogen is a polymer of β-D-glucose units joined only by C1–C4 glycosidic linkages.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Carbohydrates are stored in the animal body as glycogen, and it is called animal starch.\nThe name comes from its structure being similar to amylopectin.\nR is false. Like amylopectin, glycogen is made of α-D-glucose units and is highly branched, so it has C1–C6 branch points as well as C1–C4 links.\nA chain of β-glucose units joined only by C1–C4 links describes cellulose.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an INCORRECT pairing of a reaction of glucose with the structural conclusion drawn from it?","image":"","options":["Prolonged heating with HI gives n-hexane: the six carbon atoms form a straight chain","Oxidation with nitric acid gives saccharic acid: glucose contains a carbonyl group","Oxidation with bromine water gives gluconic acid: the carbonyl group is aldehydic","Acetylation with acetic anhydride gives a stable pentaacetate: five –OH groups on different carbons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Option (1) is correct. Formation of n-hexane shows the six carbons are in a straight chain.\nOption (3) is correct. Bromine water oxidises –CHO to –COOH without changing the carbon count, which proves an aldehyde group.\nOption (4) is correct. A stable pentaacetate shows five –OH groups on different carbons.\nOption (2) is the incorrect pairing. HNO$_3$ oxidises the terminal –CH$_2$OH as well, giving a dicarboxylic acid, which shows a primary alcoholic group. The carbonyl group is shown by oxime and cyanohydrin formation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Amylose\n(b) Amylopectin\n(c) Cellulose\n(d) Glycogen\nList-II\n(i) Straight chain of β-D-glucose units with C1–C4 links\n(ii) Highly branched; stored in liver, muscles and brain\n(iii) Unbranched α-D-glucose chain; water soluble\n(iv) Water insoluble; C1–C4 chain with C1–C6 branching","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Amylose is an unbranched, water-soluble α-glucose chain, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Amylopectin is water insoluble and branches through C1–C6 links, so it matches (iv).\n(c) Cellulose is a straight chain of β-D-glucose with C1–C4 links, so it matches (i).\n(d) Glycogen is highly branched and stored in liver, muscles and brain, so it matches (ii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which option correctly describes amylose, a component of starch?","image":"","options":["Water insoluble; about 80-85% of starch","Water soluble; about 80-85% of starch","Water soluble; about 15-20% of starch","Water insoluble; about 15-20% of starch"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Amylose is the water-soluble component of starch.\nIt makes up only about 15-20% of starch.\nIt is a long unbranched chain of 200-1000 α-D-(+)-glucose units joined by C1–C4 glycosidic links.\nThe 80-85% water-insoluble fraction is amylopectin, which is the tempting wrong choice.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about carbohydrate linkages:\n(i) Amylose is a water-soluble, unbranched chain of $\\alpha$-D-glucose units joined by C1–C4 glycosidic linkages.\n(ii) In amylopectin, branching occurs through C1–C6 glycosidic linkages.\n(iii) Cellulose is a straight-chain polymer of $\\beta$-D-glucose units joined by C1–C4 glycosidic linkages.\n(iv) Glycogen is less branched than amylopectin.\n(v) In lactose, C1 of $\\beta$-D-galactose is linked to C4 of $\\beta$-D-glucose.\n(vi) In maltose, C1 of one $\\alpha$-D-glucose is linked to C4 of another, and a free aldehyde can form at C1 of the second unit.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["4","6","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: amylose is about 15–20% of starch, soluble, unbranched, with C1–C4 links.\n(ii) Correct: amylopectin chains use C1–C4 links and branch through C1–C6 links.\n(iii) Correct: cellulose is made only of $\\beta$-D-glucose with C1–C4 links.\n(iv) Incorrect: glycogen resembles amylopectin but is more highly branched.\n(v) Correct: lactose has a galactose C1 to glucose C4 linkage.\n(vi) Correct: this free aldehyde makes maltose a reducing sugar.\nTotal correct = 5.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Lactose, the disaccharide found in milk, gives which monosaccharides on hydrolysis?","image":"","options":["Two molecules of α-D-glucose","β-D-galactose and β-D-glucose","α-D-glucose and β-D-fructose","β-D-galactose and β-D-fructose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Lactose (milk sugar) is made of β-D-galactose and β-D-glucose, joined between C1 of galactose and C4 of glucose.\nHydrolysis therefore gives galactose and glucose.\nTwo α-D-glucose units make up maltose.\nα-D-glucose with β-D-fructose makes up sucrose.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Maltose is a disaccharide in which:","image":"","options":["C1 of α-D-glucose is linked to C2 of β-D-fructose","C1 of β-D-galactose is linked to C4 of β-D-glucose","C1 of one α-D-glucose unit is linked to C4 of another α-D-glucose unit","C1 of one β-D-glucose unit is linked to C6 of another β-D-glucose unit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, maltose consists of two α-D-glucose units.\nC1 of the first glucose unit is joined to C4 of the second glucose unit.\nThe C1–C2 (glucose–fructose) link describes sucrose.\nThe galactose–glucose C1–C4 link describes lactose.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Glucose does not give Schiff's test and does not form an addition product with $NaHSO_3$.\nReason (R): Glucose does not contain a carbonyl group in any of its forms.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Assertion: true. These are two of the facts that the open-chain structure could not explain.\nReason: false. Glucose mostly exists as a cyclic hemiacetal, in which the –CHO has combined with the C-5 –OH, so the free aldehyde concentration is very small.\nHowever, the cyclic forms are in equilibrium with the open-chain form, which does contain –CHO. That is why glucose still forms an oxime and a cyanohydrin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Commercially, glucose is obtained by hydrolysing starch under which conditions?","image":"","options":["Boiling with dilute $H_2SO_4$ at 393 K under 2–3 atm pressure","Heating with concentrated $HNO_3$ at 373 K","Boiling with dilute HCl in alcoholic solution at room pressure","Heating with HI and red phosphorus at 393 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: starch, $(C_6H_{10}O_5)_n$, is boiled with dilute $H_2SO_4$ at 393 K under 2–3 atm. This gives n molecules of glucose.\nDilute HCl in alcoholic solution is the method used for sucrose, not starch. That process gives glucose and fructose.\nConcentrated $HNO_3$ oxidises glucose, and HI reduces it, so neither prepares it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The two strands of the DNA double helix are not identical but complementary to each other.\nReason (R): Hydrogen bonds in DNA are formed only between specific base pairs, adenine with thymine and cytosine with guanine.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In the Watson–Crick model, two chains are wound about each other and are complementary.\nR is true. A pairs with T and C pairs with G through hydrogen bonds.\nThis specific pairing is the reason the strands are complementary. Knowing the base on one strand fixes the base opposite it.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A nucleoside is converted into a nucleotide when phosphoric acid gets linked to which position of the sugar moiety?","image":"","options":["1′","2′","3′","5′"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A nucleoside is formed when a base attaches to the 1′ position of the pentose sugar.\nA nucleotide is formed when that nucleoside links with phosphoric acid at the 5′ position of the sugar.\nThe 1′ position is already occupied by the base.\nThe 3′ carbon takes part later, when nucleotides join through phosphodiester linkages.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following observations about glucose could NOT be explained by its open-chain structure?\n(a) It does not give Schiff's test\n(b) It does not form a hydrogensulphite addition product with NaHSO$_3$\n(c) Its pentaacetate does not react with hydroxylamine\n(d) On prolonged heating with HI it gives n-hexane\n(e) It exists in two crystalline forms, α and β\n(f) Bromine water oxidises it to gluconic acid\n(g) It forms an oxime with hydroxylamine","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Not explained. A free –CHO group should give Schiff's test.\n(b) Not explained. The expected NaHSO$_3$ adduct does not form.\n(c) Not explained. This shows there is no free –CHO group in the pentaacetate.\n(d) Explained. It supports a straight chain of six carbons.\n(e) Not explained. The two forms arise only from the cyclic hemiacetal.\n(f) Explained. It shows the carbonyl is an aldehyde.\n(g) Explained. It shows a carbonyl group is present.\nSo the unexplained ones are a, b, c and e, which makes 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (monosaccharide) with List-II (classification).\nList-I:\n(A) Glyceraldehyde\n(B) Ribose\n(C) Fructose\n(D) Glucose\nList-II:\n(I) Aldohexose\n(II) Ketohexose\n(III) Aldopentose\n(IV) Aldotriose","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Glyceraldehyde, $CHO-CHOH-CH_2OH$, has 3 carbons and an aldehyde group, so it is an aldotriose (IV).\n(B) Ribose has 5 carbons and a $-CHO$ group, so it is an aldopentose (III).\n(C) Fructose has 6 carbons and a keto group at C-2, so it is a ketohexose (II).\n(D) Glucose has 6 carbons and a $-CHO$ group, so it is an aldohexose (I).\nOption (2) wrongly swaps glucose and fructose, which have the same formula $C_6H_{12}O_6$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Epinephrine, thyroxine and endorphins all belong to the same chemical class of hormones.\nStatement II: Testosterone and estradiol are steroid hormones produced by the gonads.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. Epinephrine is an amino acid derivative, and thyroxine is also an amino acid derivative (an iodinated form of tyrosine). Endorphins, however, are polypeptides like insulin, so the three do not share one chemical class.\nStatement II is true. The gonads (testes and ovaries) produce steroid hormones. Testosterone is the major male sex hormone and estradiol is the main female sex hormone.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A patient shows fissuring at the corners of the mouth and lips with a burning sensation of the skin, and also has bleeding gums. Which statement correctly describes the two vitamins most likely deficient?","image":"","options":["Both are fat-soluble and are stored in the liver, so deficiency appears only after very long deprivation","One is fat-soluble and the other water-soluble; only the fat-soluble one needs regular dietary supply","Both belong to the B group and can be stored in the body like vitamin B12","Both are water-soluble, are readily excreted in urine and must be supplied regularly in the diet"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Fissuring at the mouth corners and a burning sensation of the skin point to cheilosis, caused by vitamin B2 (riboflavin) deficiency.\nBleeding gums point to scurvy, caused by vitamin C deficiency.\nB2 and C are both water-soluble vitamins.\nThey are readily excreted in urine and are not stored (only B12 is an exception), so they must be taken regularly.\nOption (3) fails because C is not a B-group vitamin and neither can be stored.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Monosaccharide)\n(a) Glyceraldehyde\n(b) Fructose\n(c) Ribose\n(d) Glucose\nList-II (Class)\n(i) Aldopentose\n(ii) Aldohexose\n(iii) Ketohexose\n(iv) Aldotriose","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Glyceraldehyde has 3 C atoms and a –CHO group, so it is an aldotriose (iv).\n(b) Fructose has 6 C atoms and a keto group at C2, so it is a ketohexose (iii).\n(c) Ribose has 5 C atoms and a –CHO group, so it is an aldopentose (i).\n(d) Glucose has 6 C atoms and a –CHO group, so it is an aldohexose (ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Amino acids are high melting crystalline solids that are soluble in water, unlike simple amines or carboxylic acids of comparable mass.\nReason (R): Amino acids exist as dipolar zwitter ions, which gives them salt-like character.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT describes amino acids as colourless, crystalline, water-soluble, high melting solids.\nR is true. The acidic –COOH group and the basic –NH$_2$ group in the same molecule form the zwitter ion $H_3N^+-CHR-COO^-$.\nR explains A. Strong ionic attractions between zwitter ions raise the melting point, and ion–dipole interactions with water make them soluble, just as for salts.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Saccharic acid obtained from glucose has the molecular formula $C_6H_{10}O_8$.\n(ii) Gluconic acid has the molecular formula $C_6H_{12}O_7$.\n(iii) The cyanohydrin formed by adding HCN to glucose contains seven carbon atoms.\n(iv) Bromine water oxidises the terminal $CH_2OH$ group of glucose to –COOH.\n(v) In the Fischer projection of D-glucose (–CHO at top), the –OH group on C-3 lies on the left.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: –CHO becomes –COOH (+O) and –$CH_2OH$ becomes –COOH (–2H, +O). So $C_6H_{12}O_6$ becomes $C_6H_{10}O_8$.\n(ii) Correct: only –CHO becomes –COOH, giving $C_6H_{12}O_7$.\n(iii) Correct: HCN adds one carbon (–CN), giving seven carbons.\n(iv) Incorrect: bromine water oxidises only the –CHO group. The $CH_2OH$ group is oxidised by $HNO_3$.\n(v) Correct: the –OH groups on C-2 to C-5 lie right, left, right, right.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Cellulose, the main constituent of plant cell walls, is a straight-chain polymer made only of","image":"","options":["α-D-glucose units","β-D-fructose units","α-D-galactose units","β-D-glucose units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT describes cellulose as a straight-chain polysaccharide made only of β-D-glucose units.\nThese units are joined by C1–C4 glycosidic linkages.\nα-D-glucose is the unit of starch and glycogen, not cellulose. This is the most common confusion.\nFructose and galactose are not monomers of cellulose.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"DNA fingerprinting is based on the fact that the sequence of bases in DNA is unique for a person. Which of the following is NOT a use of DNA fingerprinting mentioned in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Identification of criminals in forensic laboratories","Determining the paternity of an individual","Identifying dead bodies in an accident by comparing DNA of parents or children","Curing hereditary diseases by changing the base sequence"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four uses of DNA fingerprinting:\n(i) identifying criminals, (ii) determining paternity, (iii) identifying dead bodies, and (iv) identifying racial groups.\nDNA fingerprinting only reads and compares base sequences. The base sequence cannot be altered by any known treatment.\nSo curing diseases by changing the sequence is not one of its uses.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"The six-membered cyclic structure of glucose is called the pyranose structure. This name comes from pyran, which is a cyclic compound whose ring contains:","image":"","options":["six carbon atoms and no oxygen","four carbon atoms and one oxygen atom","five carbon atoms and one oxygen atom","five carbon atoms and one nitrogen atom"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Pyran is a six-membered ring made of one oxygen atom and five carbon atoms.\nThe glucose ring has the same make-up: C-1 to C-5 plus the ring oxygen that came from the C-5 –OH.\nOption (2), four carbons and one oxygen, describes furan, which is the basis of the furanose ring of fructose.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Prolonged heating of glucose with HI gives n-hexane. What does this observation establish about glucose?","image":"","options":["Glucose contains an aldehyde group","Glucose has five hydroxyl groups","All six carbon atoms of glucose are linked in a straight chain","Glucose contains a primary alcoholic group"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"HI removes all the oxygen-containing groups and leaves the hydrocarbon skeleton.\nThe product is n-hexane, an unbranched $C_6$ alkane. So the six carbons of glucose form a straight chain.\nThe aldehyde group is shown by oxidation with bromine water. The five –OH groups are shown by acetylation. The primary –OH is shown by oxidation with $HNO_3$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"In aqueous solution, an α-amino acid exists as a zwitter ion. In this dipolar ion:","image":"","options":["the amino group loses a proton and the carboxyl group gains a proton","the carboxyl group loses a proton and the amino group accepts a proton","both the amino and carboxyl groups lose protons","both groups remain un-ionised, but the molecule becomes polar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The –COOH group is acidic, so it loses $H^+$ and becomes $-COO^-$.\nThe –NH$_2$ group is basic, so it accepts $H^+$ and becomes $-NH_3^+$.\nThe resulting ion $H_3N^+-CHR-COO^-$ carries both charges but is neutral overall.\nOption (1) reverses the acid and base roles of the two groups.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which reaction of glucose confirms that it contains five –OH groups?","image":"","options":["Reaction with hydroxylamine forming an oxime","Addition of HCN forming a cyanohydrin","Oxidation with nitric acid forming saccharic acid","Acetylation with acetic anhydride forming a pentaacetate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Each –OH group is acetylated by acetic anhydride. The product is glucose pentaacetate, which shows that there are five –OH groups.\nThe pentaacetate is stable, so these –OH groups must be on different carbon atoms.\nThe oxime and cyanohydrin reactions show a carbonyl group. Oxidation with $HNO_3$ shows a primary –OH group.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement correctly describes the amount of enzyme needed for a biochemical reaction to progress?","image":"","options":["Enzymes are needed in stoichiometric amounts equal to the substrate","Enzymes are needed only in small quantities","Enzymes must be present in large excess over the substrate","The required amount of enzyme equals the amount of product formed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that enzymes are needed only in small quantities for a reaction to progress.\nLike chemical catalysts, an enzyme is not used up, so one enzyme molecule can act on substrate again and again.\nThe other options wrongly treat the enzyme as a reactant that is consumed in fixed proportion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"When egg white is boiled, its proteins coagulate. Which level of protein structure remains intact during this change?","image":"","options":["Secondary structure","Tertiary structure","Quaternary structure","Primary structure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Coagulation of egg white on boiling is an example of denaturation.\nHeat disturbs the hydrogen bonds, so the globules unfold and the helices uncoil. The secondary and tertiary structures are therefore destroyed.\nThe peptide bonds are not broken. The amino acid sequence, which is the primary structure, stays intact.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes fructose?","image":"","options":["An aldohexose of the D-series that is dextrorotatory","A ketohexose with its keto group at C-2, belonging to the D-series and laevorotatory","A ketohexose with its keto group at C-3, belonging to the L-series","A ketopentose that forms a six-membered pyranose ring"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fructose has the formula $C_6H_{12}O_6$, a straight six-carbon chain and a keto group at C-2, so it is a ketohexose.\nIt belongs to the D-series but is laevorotatory, so its correct name is D-(–)-fructose.\nIts cyclic forms are five-membered furanose rings, which rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Starch is made of two components. Which component is water soluble and consists of a long unbranched chain of $\\alpha$-D-(+)-glucose units held together only by C1–C4 glycosidic linkages?","image":"","options":["Amylopectin","Amylose","Glycogen","Cellulose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Starch is a polymer of $\\alpha$-glucose made of amylose and amylopectin.\nAmylose is the water-soluble part. It makes up about 15–20% of starch and is an unbranched chain of 200–1000 $\\alpha$-D-glucose units joined by C1–C4 links, so it fits.\nAmylopectin (80–85% of starch) is insoluble in water and branched through C1–C6 links, so it does not fit.\nGlycogen is a highly branched animal polysaccharide.\nCellulose is built from $\\beta$-D-glucose, not $\\alpha$-glucose.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following amino acids are essential amino acids?\nValine, Glycine, Lysine, Serine, Tryptophan, Alanine, Histidine, Tyrosine","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Essential amino acids cannot be synthesised in the body and must come from the diet.\nEssential ones in this list: valine, lysine, tryptophan and histidine.\nNon-essential ones in this list: glycine, serine, alanine and tyrosine. Tyrosine is a tempting trap because phenylalanine is essential, but tyrosine is not marked essential in NCERT.\nSo the count is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Glycogen is called 'animal starch'. This name comes from the fact that its structure resembles which starch component?","image":"","options":["Amylose, but glycogen is less branched","Amylopectin, but glycogen is more highly branched","Cellulose, but glycogen is made of α-units","Amylose, but glycogen has C1–C6 linkages only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says glycogen is called animal starch because its structure is similar to amylopectin.\nGlycogen is even more highly branched than amylopectin.\nAmylose is an unbranched chain, so it is not the component glycogen resembles.\nCellulose is a structural polysaccharide made of β-glucose, not a storage starch.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Sucrose reduces Tollens' reagent.\n(ii) Lactose is known as milk sugar and is a reducing sugar.\n(iii) In maltose, the glycosidic linkage joins C1 of one glucose unit to C4 of the other.\n(iv) Hydrolysis of sucrose gives glucose and fructose in a 1:1 molar ratio.\n(v) Lactose on hydrolysis gives two molecules of glucose.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: both reducing groups in sucrose are bonded, so it is non-reducing.\n(ii) Correct: a free aldehyde group can form at C1 of the glucose unit.\n(iii) Correct: maltose has an α C1–C4 link.\n(iv) Correct: hydrolysis gives an equimolar mixture of glucose and fructose.\n(v) Incorrect: lactose gives galactose and glucose.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"The specific rotations of D-glucose and D-fructose are $+52.5^\\circ$ and $-92.4^\\circ$ respectively. Sucrose is hydrolysed completely to an equimolar mixture of the two. Both are $C_6H_{12}O_6$, so equal masses are present. What is the approximate specific rotation of the resulting invert sugar?","image":"","options":["$+19.95^\\circ$","$-39.9^\\circ$","$-19.95^\\circ$","$-92.4^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: [α] of glucose = +52.5°, [α] of fructose = −92.4°. The two are present in equal masses (mass fraction 0.5 each).\nFormula: [α] of mixture = 0.5 × [α] of glucose + 0.5 × [α] of fructose.\nSubstituting: [α] = 0.5(52.5) + 0.5(−92.4) = −19.95°.\nSo the invert sugar is laevorotatory, about −19.95°. The sign changes from + (sucrose) to −.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the open-chain Fischer structure of D-(+)-glucose (–CHO written at the top) are correct?\n(i) The –OH group on C-3 lies on the left-hand side.\n(ii) Glucose is assigned the D-configuration because the –OH on C-5 lies on the right, as in (+)-glyceraldehyde.\n(iii) The letter 'D' indicates that glucose is dextrorotatory.\n(iv) In saccharic acid formed by $HNO_3$ oxidation, both C-1 and C-6 are –COOH groups, while the configuration at C-2 to C-5 is unchanged.\n(v) The open-chain structure has four asymmetric carbon atoms.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the –OH groups on C-2 to C-5 lie right, left, right, right.\n(ii) Correct: the lowest asymmetric carbon (C-5) is compared with (+)-glyceraldehyde.\n(iii) Incorrect: D shows relative configuration only and has no relation to optical rotation. The sign (+) shows dextrorotation.\n(iv) Correct: $HNO_3$ oxidises both –CHO and –CH$_2$OH to –COOH, and C-2 to C-5 are untouched.\n(v) Correct: C-2, C-3, C-4 and C-5 are asymmetric.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A solution is prepared by dissolving 34.2 g of sucrose in water to make 100 mL. It is placed in a 1 dm polarimeter tube and hydrolysed with dilute acid. The specific rotations are: sucrose $+66.5^\\circ$, D-glucose $+52.5^\\circ$, D-fructose $-92.4^\\circ$. Specific rotation is defined as $[\\alpha] = \\frac{\\alpha}{l \\times c}$, with $l$ in dm and $c$ in g/mL. Molar masses: sucrose = 342 g/mol, glucose = fructose = 180 g/mol. Ignore the very small change in volume. What percentage of the sucrose must have been hydrolysed when the observed rotation first becomes zero?","image":"","options":["50.0%","63.2%","76.0%","85.4%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 0.1 mol of sucrose in 100 mL. Let the fraction hydrolysed be x. Each mole of sucrose gives 1 mol of glucose and 1 mol of fructose.\nAmounts present: sucrose = 0.342(1−x) g/mL; glucose = fructose = 0.18x g/mL each.\nFormula: total rotation $\\alpha = l\\sum [\\alpha]_i c_i$.\nSubstituting: $66.5(0.342)(1-x) + (52.5-92.4)(0.18x) = 0$, which gives $22.74(1-x) = 7.18x$.\nSo x = 22.74/29.92 ≈ 0.760, that is, 76.0% hydrolysed. Beyond this point the strong laevorotation of fructose makes the mixture laevorotatory, producing invert sugar.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Vitamin C cannot be stored in the human body and must be taken regularly in the diet.\nReason (R): Vitamin C is a fat-soluble vitamin that is deposited in adipose tissue.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Assertion: true. Vitamin C is readily excreted in urine and cannot be stored, so it must be supplied regularly in the diet.\nReason: false. Vitamin C is water-soluble, not fat-soluble.\nOnly the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K are stored in liver and adipose tissue.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When egg white coagulates on boiling, the secondary and tertiary structures of its proteins are destroyed, but the sequence of amino acids remains unchanged.\nStatement II: In the $\\alpha$-helix, separate polypeptide chains lying side by side are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds between –NH– and >C=O groups.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Coagulation of egg white is denaturation. Heat disturbs the hydrogen bonds, so the helices uncoil and the globules unfold. The secondary and tertiary structures are lost, but the primary structure (amino acid sequence) stays intact.\nStatement II is incorrect. In the $\\alpha$-helix, a single chain twists into a right-handed helix. The –NH of each residue hydrogen-bonds to a >C=O of an adjacent turn of the same chain.\nChains laid side by side and held by intermolecular hydrogen bonds describe the $\\beta$-pleated sheet, not the $\\alpha$-helix.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (level of protein structure) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\n(A) Primary structure\n(B) Secondary structure\n(C) Tertiary structure\n(D) Quaternary structure\nList-II:\n(I) Spatial arrangement of two or more polypeptide sub-units with respect to each other\n(II) Specific sequence of amino acids in a polypeptide chain\n(III) Overall folding of the polypeptide chain, giving fibrous or globular shape\n(IV) α-helix or β-pleated sheet arising from hydrogen bonding between C=O and –NH– groups of peptide bonds","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The primary structure is the amino acid sequence, so A matches II.\n(B) The secondary structure is the α-helix or β-pleated sheet, formed by hydrogen bonds between the >C=O and –NH– groups of the backbone, so B matches IV.\n(C) The tertiary structure is the further folding of the secondary structure into fibrous or globular shapes, so C matches III.\n(D) The quaternary structure is the arrangement of sub-units, as in haemoglobin, so D matches I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Vitamin C must be supplied regularly in our diet.\nReason (R): Water-soluble vitamins are readily excreted in urine and, except vitamin $B_{12}$, cannot be stored in the body.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: vitamin C (ascorbic acid) has to come from food such as citrus fruits, amla and green leafy vegetables, and it has to be eaten regularly.\nR is true: NCERT states that B-group vitamins and vitamin C are water soluble. They are readily excreted in urine and cannot be stored, with vitamin $B_{12}$ as the only exception.\nVitamin C is water soluble and is not stored, so R directly explains why it must be supplied regularly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following vitamins can be stored in the human body?\nVitamin A, Vitamin B$_1$, Vitamin B$_2$, Vitamin B$_{12}$, Vitamin C, Vitamin D, Vitamin E, Vitamin K","image":"","options":["4","5","3","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K are stored in the liver and adipose tissue. That gives 4.\nWater-soluble vitamins (B group and C) are readily excreted in urine and cannot be stored, with one exception: vitamin B$_{12}$.\nVitamins B$_1$, B$_2$ and C cannot be stored.\nTotal that can be stored = 4 + 1 = 5.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (polysaccharide) with List-II (feature).\nList-I:\n(a) Amylose\n(b) Amylopectin\n(c) Cellulose\n(d) Glycogen\nList-II:\n(i) Straight chain of $\\beta$-D-glucose units linked C1–C4; main constituent of plant cell walls\n(ii) Water-insoluble component forming 80–85% of starch, with C1–C6 branching\n(iii) Water-soluble, unbranched component forming 15–20% of starch\n(iv) Storage carbohydrate in liver, muscles and brain; even more highly branched than amylopectin\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Amylose matches (iii): it is soluble in water, unbranched, and forms about 15–20% of starch.\n(b) Amylopectin matches (ii): it is insoluble in water, forms 80–85% of starch, and branches through C1–C6 links.\n(c) Cellulose matches (i): it is a straight chain of $\\beta$-D-glucose with C1–C4 links and is the main constituent of plant cell walls.\n(d) Glycogen matches (iv): it is called animal starch, is stored in liver, muscles and brain, and is more highly branched than amylopectin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"To assign the D or L configuration of a monosaccharide, its structure is compared with glyceraldehyde. Which carbon atom of glucose is used for this comparison?","image":"","options":["C-1, the aldehyde carbon","C-2, the asymmetric carbon next to –CHO","C-5, the lowest asymmetric carbon","All four asymmetric carbons taken together"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rule: write the structure with the most oxidised carbon (–CHO) at the top. Then compare only the lowest asymmetric carbon with glyceraldehyde.\nIn glucose the asymmetric carbons are C-2 to C-5, so the lowest one is C-5.\nThe –OH on C-5 lies on the right, as in D-(+)-glyceraldehyde, so glucose is a D-sugar.\nC-1 is not asymmetric in the open chain. Option (2) is wrong because the other asymmetric carbons are not considered.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Glucose forms a stable pentaacetate, which shows that its five –OH groups are attached to five different carbon atoms.\nReason (R): A compound having two –OH groups on the same carbon atom is generally unstable and readily loses a water molecule.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Acetylation gives a stable glucose pentaacetate, which confirms five –OH groups. Because the compound is stable, these groups must sit on different carbons.\nR is true. A gem-diol (two –OH groups on one carbon) usually loses water to form a carbonyl compound.\nR explains A. If any carbon carried two –OH groups, the molecule would not be stable.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A person whose diet has lacked citrus fruits, amla and green leafy vegetables for a long time develops bleeding gums. The disease and the vitamin involved are:","image":"","options":["Scurvy; vitamin C (ascorbic acid)","Beri beri; vitamin B1 (thiamine)","Rickets; vitamin D","Pernicious anaemia; vitamin B12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Citrus fruits, amla and green leafy vegetables are sources of vitamin C (ascorbic acid).\nVitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, whose main sign is bleeding gums.\nBeri beri causes loss of appetite and retarded growth.\nRickets causes bone deformities in children.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): α-Amino acids are high melting crystalline solids that behave more like salts than like simple amines or carboxylic acids.\nReason (R): In aqueous solution, the –COOH group of an amino acid loses a proton and the –$NH_2$ group gains a proton, forming a dipolar zwitter ion.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Amino acids are colourless, water-soluble, high melting crystalline solids with salt-like behaviour.\nR is true. The carboxyl group gives up a proton and the amino group accepts one, which produces the zwitter ion.\nThe zwitter ion carries both a + and a – charge. This leads to strong ionic (electrostatic) attractions between neighbouring molecules, which explains the high melting points and the salt-like nature.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are reducing sugars?\nGlucose, fructose, sucrose, maltose, lactose, ribose","image":"","options":["4","5","3","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"All monosaccharides, whether aldose or ketose, are reducing. So glucose, fructose and ribose count.\nMaltose and lactose can regenerate a free aldehyde group at C1 of a glucose unit, so both are reducing.\nSucrose is non-reducing because the reducing groups of both units are used in the glycosidic bond.\nTotal reducing sugars = 5.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Glycine is the only naturally occurring $\\alpha$-amino acid that is optically inactive.\nReason (R): Most naturally occurring $\\alpha$-amino acids have L-configuration.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. In glycine the side chain R = H, so the $\\alpha$-carbon carries two H atoms and is not asymmetric. Every other natural $\\alpha$-amino acid has an asymmetric $\\alpha$-carbon.\nR is also true, since most natural amino acids are L-isomers.\nHowever, the L-configuration of other amino acids does not explain why glycine lacks optical activity. The real reason is the absence of a chiral carbon.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Glucose pentaacetate does not react with $NH_2OH$\n(B) Glucose exists in two crystalline forms (m.p. 419 K and 423 K)\n(C) The 'D' in D-glucose\n(D) The '(–)' in D-(–)-fructose\nList-II\n(i) –OH on the lowest asymmetric carbon lies on the right side\n(ii) Rotates plane-polarised light to the left\n(iii) No free –CHO group is present\n(iv) Anomers that differ in configuration at C-1","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): the pentaacetate does not react with hydroxylamine, so it has no free –CHO group.\nB-(iv): the two forms are the α- and β-anomers, which differ only at C-1.\nC-(i): 'D' means the –OH on the lowest asymmetric carbon (C-5) is on the right, as in D-(+)-glyceraldehyde.\nD-(ii): '(–)' means laevorotatory.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (level of protein structure) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\n(a) Primary\n(b) Secondary\n(c) Tertiary\n(d) Quaternary\nList-II:\n(p) Spatial arrangement of two or more polypeptide sub-units, as in haemoglobin\n(q) Specific sequence of amino acids in the chain\n(r) Overall folding of the chain, giving fibrous or globular shapes\n(s) Regular folding of the backbone into α-helix or β-pleated sheet through H-bonding","image":"","options":["(a)-q, (b)-s, (c)-r, (d)-p","(a)-q, (b)-r, (c)-s, (d)-p","(a)-s, (b)-q, (c)-r, (d)-p","(a)-q, (b)-s, (c)-p, (d)-r"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Primary structure is the amino acid sequence, so it matches (q).\n(b) Secondary structure is the α-helix or β-pleated sheet formed by H-bonds between C=O and –NH groups, so it matches (s).\n(c) Tertiary structure is the further, overall folding that gives fibrous or globular shapes, so it matches (r).\n(d) Quaternary structure is the arrangement of sub-units, as in haemoglobin, so it matches (p).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the trivial names of amino acids.\nStatement I: Glycine gets its name from the Greek word glykos, which means sweet, because glycine tastes sweet.\nStatement II: Tyrosine gets its name from the Greek word tyros, which means cheese, because it was first obtained from cheese.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that the trivial names of α-amino acids usually reflect a property or the source of the compound.\nStatement I is correct: glycine is sweet, and glykos means sweet.\nStatement II is correct: tyrosine was first isolated from cheese, and tyros means cheese.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the α-helix structure of proteins is correct?","image":"","options":["It is a left-handed helix stabilised by disulphide bonds only","It is a right-handed helix in which the –NH group of each residue is hydrogen bonded to a C=O group of an adjacent turn","It is formed when fully stretched chains lie side by side, joined by intermolecular hydrogen bonds","It represents the sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide chain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In an α-helix, the polypeptide chain twists into a right-handed screw. The –NH of each amino acid residue is hydrogen bonded to a C=O of the adjacent turn.\nOption (1) is wrong on both counts: the helix is right-handed, and it is held mainly by hydrogen bonds.\nOption (3) describes the β-pleated sheet.\nOption (4) describes the primary structure.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Maltase\n(B) Sucrase\n(C) Oxidoreductase\n(D) Acid ($H^+$) catalysed hydrolysis of sucrose\nList-II\n(I) Activation energy = 6.22 kJ $mol^{-1}$\n(II) Converts one disaccharide molecule into two molecules of glucose\n(III) Activation energy = 2.15 kJ $mol^{-1}$\n(IV) Oxidation of one substrate with simultaneous reduction of another","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Maltase hydrolyses maltose into 2 glucose molecules, so A-II.\n(B) Sucrase-catalysed hydrolysis of sucrose has activation energy 2.15 kJ/mol, so B-III.\n(C) Oxidoreductases catalyse oxidation of one substrate with simultaneous reduction of another, so C-IV.\n(D) Acid hydrolysis of sucrose has activation energy 6.22 kJ/mol, so D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Chemically, the peptide linkage that joins α-amino acids in proteins is best described as:","image":"","options":["An ester linkage, –CO–O–, formed between –COOH and –OH groups","An amide linkage, –CO–NH–, formed between –COOH and –NH$_2$ groups with loss of water","A glycosidic linkage, –O–, formed between two hydroxyl groups","A phosphodiester linkage formed between two hydroxyl groups and phosphoric acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines the peptide linkage as an amide formed between the –COOH group of one amino acid and the –NH$_2$ group of another.\nA water molecule is eliminated, and the bond formed is –CO–NH–.\nAn ester linkage needs an –OH group, not an amino group. Glycosidic linkages join sugars, and phosphodiester linkages join nucleotides.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following conditions are caused by deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins?\nXerophthalmia, night blindness, rickets, osteomalacia, scurvy, beri beri, increased fragility of RBCs, cheilosis, increased blood clotting time, convulsions","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The fat-soluble vitamins are A, D, E and K.\nVitamin A deficiency causes xerophthalmia and night blindness (2).\nVitamin D deficiency causes rickets and osteomalacia (2).\nVitamin E deficiency causes increased fragility of RBCs (1).\nVitamin K deficiency causes increased blood clotting time (1).\nScurvy (C), beri beri (B1), cheilosis (B2) and convulsions (B6) come from water-soluble vitamins. Total = 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Insulin, which keeps the blood glucose level within narrow limits, is a steroid hormone.\nStatement II: Thyroxine, produced in the thyroid gland, is an iodinated derivative of the amino acid tyrosine.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. NCERT classes insulin (with endorphins) as a polypeptide hormone. Estrogens and androgens are the steroid examples.\nStatement II is correct. Thyroxine is an iodinated derivative of tyrosine. Low thyroxine causes hypothyroidism.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match each observation on glucose in List-I with the conclusion it supports in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Prolonged heating with HI gives n-hexane\n(B) Forms an oxime with hydroxylamine\n(C) Oxidised to gluconic acid by bromine water\n(D) Forms a stable pentaacetate with acetic anhydride\nList-II\n(I) Carbonyl group is present as an aldehyde group\n(II) Five –OH groups on different carbon atoms\n(III) Six carbon atoms in a straight chain\n(IV) Presence of a >C=O group","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Reduction with HI gives n-hexane, which shows the six carbons form an unbranched chain. A matches III.\n(B) Oxime formation (like cyanohydrin formation) proves only that a carbonyl group is present. B matches IV.\n(C) A mild oxidant converts glucose into a six-carbon acid, so the carbonyl must be an aldehyde. C matches I.\n(D) A stable pentaacetate means five –OH groups, each on a different carbon. D matches II.\nThe tempting option 2 wrongly links B to the aldehyde. An oxime forms from ketones as well, so it cannot identify an aldehyde.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following hormones are steroids?\nEstrogens, androgens, insulin, endorphins, testosterone, estradiol, epinephrine, glucocorticoids","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Steroids: estrogens and androgens are NCERT's own examples. Testosterone and estradiol are gonadal steroid hormones. Glucocorticoids come from the adrenal cortex, which produces steroid hormones. That gives 5.\nNot steroids: insulin and endorphins are polypeptides. Epinephrine is an amino acid derivative.\nTotal = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the structure of proteins is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Insulin, with only 51 amino acids, is called a protein because it has a well-defined protein conformation","In an $\\alpha$-helix, the polypeptide twists into a right-handed screw with the –NH group of each residue hydrogen bonded to a C=O of an adjacent turn","In a $\\beta$-pleated sheet, a single chain folds on itself and is held only by intramolecular hydrogen bonds","The spatial arrangement of two or more polypeptide sub-units relative to each other is the quaternary structure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT cites insulin (51 amino acids) as a protein because of its well-defined conformation.\n(2) Correct: This is the NCERT description of the $\\alpha$-helix.\n(3) Incorrect: In a $\\beta$-pleated sheet, the chains are stretched to nearly maximum extension, laid side by side and held by INTERmolecular hydrogen bonds.\n(4) Correct: This is the definition of quaternary structure.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Maltose is classified as an oligosaccharide.\nStatement II: Carbohydrates that yield two to ten monosaccharide units on hydrolysis are called oligosaccharides.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is true. It is the NCERT definition of oligosaccharides (2 to 10 monosaccharide units on hydrolysis).\nStatement I is true. Maltose gives two glucose molecules on hydrolysis, so it is a disaccharide. Disaccharides are the most common type of oligosaccharide.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (amino acid) with List-II (one-letter code).\nList-I:\n(A) Glutamine\n(B) Tryptophan\n(C) Phenylalanine\n(D) Aspartic acid\nList-II:\n(I) F\n(II) D\n(III) Q\n(IV) W","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Glutamine (Gln) has the code Q. It is not G (glycine) and not E (glutamic acid).\nTryptophan (Trp) has the code W.\nPhenylalanine (Phe) has the code F.\nAspartic acid (Asp) has the code D.\nSo the matching is A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"The tripeptide Gly-Ala-Gly is formed from two molecules of glycine (molar mass 75 g/mol) and one molecule of alanine (molar mass 89 g/mol) by peptide bond formation. What is the molar mass of the tripeptide? (Molar mass of H$_2$O = 18 g/mol)","image":"","options":["239 g/mol","221 g/mol","203 g/mol","185 g/mol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: two glycine (75 g/mol each), one alanine (89 g/mol), water = 18 g/mol.\nFormula: a tripeptide has 2 peptide bonds, and each bond releases 1 H$_2$O. So M = sum of amino acid masses − 2 × 18.\nSubstituting: M = 75 + 89 + 75 − 36 = 239 − 36.\nSo M = 203 g/mol.\nThe option 239 g/mol ignores the water lost, and 221 g/mol subtracts only one water.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Sucrose is completely hydrolysed to an equimolar mixture of D-glucose and D-fructose (molar mass of each = 180 g/mol). Take the specific rotations as +52.5° for glucose and −92.4° for fructose. Assume the rotations are additive in proportion to the mass fraction of each component. What is the specific rotation of the invert sugar formed?","image":"","options":["−19.95°","+19.95°","−39.90°","−92.40°"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the mixture is equimolar and both sugars have the same molar mass, so each has a mass fraction of 0.5.\nFormula: [α]mix = 0.5 × [α]glucose + 0.5 × [α]fructose\nSubstituting: [α]mix = 0.5 × (+52.5°) + 0.5 × (−92.4°) = 26.25° − 46.2°\nSo [α]mix = −19.95°. The negative sign means the mixture is laevorotatory, which is why it is called invert sugar.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Acetic acid is not classified as a carbohydrate.\nReason (R): The molecular formula of acetic acid can be written as $C_2(H_2O)_2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Acetic acid is not an optically active polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone, so it is not a carbohydrate.\nR is true. $C_2H_4O_2$ can be written as $C_2(H_2O)_2$.\nHowever, fitting the formula $C_x(H_2O)_y$ would, if anything, suggest it IS a carbohydrate. R therefore does not explain A.\nThe correct explanation is the absence of the required functional groups.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Glucose is oxidised by bromine water, a mild oxidising agent, to a six-carbon acid. Identify the acid and the conclusion drawn from this reaction.","image":"","options":["Saccharic acid; glucose has a primary alcoholic group","Gluconic acid; the carbonyl group of glucose is aldehydic","Gluconic acid; glucose has a ketonic group","Saccharic acid; the carbonyl group of glucose is aldehydic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Bromine water oxidises only the –CHO group to –COOH. The product is gluconic acid, which still has six carbons.\nA mild oxidant cannot oxidise a ketone without breaking the carbon chain. So the carbonyl group must be aldehydic.\nSaccharic acid is formed only with the stronger oxidant $HNO_3$, which also oxidises the $CH_2OH$ end.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\n(i) Vitamin A deficiency causes both xerophthalmia and night blindness, and fish liver oil and carrots are its sources.\n(ii) Vitamin D can be obtained by exposure to sunlight, and its deficiency in adults causes osteomalacia.\n(iii) Deficiency of vitamin B6, also called riboflavin, causes convulsions.\n(iv) Pernicious anaemia, in which RBCs are deficient in haemoglobin, results from vitamin B12 deficiency.\n(v) Being water-soluble, vitamin B12, like vitamin C, cannot be stored in the body.\nThe correct statements are:","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iv) and (v) only","(i) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: vitamin A deficiency causes xerophthalmia and night blindness, and its sources include fish liver oil, carrots, butter and milk.\n(ii) Correct: sunlight is a source of vitamin D, and osteomalacia (soft bones, joint pain) occurs in adults.\n(iii) Incorrect: B6 is pyridoxine; riboflavin is B2.\n(iv) Correct.\n(v) Incorrect: B12 is the exception among water-soluble vitamins and can be stored.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Maltose gives a positive test with Tollens' reagent.\nReason (R): In maltose, the two glucose units are linked through the C1 atoms of both units.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Maltose is a reducing sugar, so it reduces Tollens' reagent.\nR is false. In maltose, C1 of one $\\alpha$-D-glucose is linked to C4 of the second glucose.\nBecause C1 of the second unit is left free, an aldehyde group can form there in solution. This is why maltose is reducing.\nIf both C1 atoms were bonded, the sugar would be non-reducing.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"An amino acid listed in NCERT Table 10.2 satisfies all three of the following:\n(i) it cannot be synthesised in the human body;\n(ii) it contains more amino groups than carboxyl groups;\n(iii) its side chain contains a straight chain of four $CH_2$ groups.\nIts one-letter code is:","image":"","options":["R","K","L","Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Condition (ii) means the amino acid is basic. In Table 10.2, lysine and arginine have extra nitrogen groups in their side chains.\nBoth lysine and arginine are essential, so both satisfy (i).\nCondition (iii) decides between them. Lysine's side chain is $H_2N-(CH_2)_4-$, which has four $CH_2$ groups. Arginine's side chain has only $(CH_2)_3$.\nLysine's one-letter code is K. R is arginine, L is leucine and Q is glutamine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Deficiency of which vitamin leads to increased blood clotting time?","image":"","options":["Vitamin A","Vitamin D","Vitamin E","Vitamin K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Table 10.3 of NCERT lists increased blood clotting time as the deficiency disease of vitamin K.\nVitamin K comes from green leafy vegetables.\nVitamin E deficiency causes increased fragility of RBCs and muscular weakness, not slow clotting.\nVitamin A deficiency causes xerophthalmia and night blindness, and vitamin D deficiency causes rickets and osteomalacia.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction of glucose) with List-II (structural conclusion).\nList-I:\n(A) Reaction with hydroxylamine\n(B) Oxidation with bromine water to gluconic acid\n(C) Acetylation with acetic anhydride\n(D) Prolonged heating with HI\nList-II:\n(i) Presence of five –OH groups\n(ii) Six carbon atoms in a straight chain\n(iii) Presence of a carbonyl group\n(iv) The carbonyl group is aldehydic\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Hydroxylamine forms an oxime, which shows a >C=O group: (iii).\n(B) Bromine water, a mild oxidant, gives a six-carbon acid with the same number of carbons, so the carbonyl is a –CHO group: (iv).\n(C) Glucose pentaacetate forms, which shows five –OH groups: (i).\n(D) n-Hexane forms, which shows a straight six-carbon chain: (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"What is the one-letter code for the amino acid lysine?","image":"","options":["L","Y","K","S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In Table 10.2, lysine (side chain $H_2N-(CH_2)_4-$) has the three-letter symbol Lys and the one-letter code K.\nThe letter L is already used for leucine.\nY stands for tyrosine and S for serine.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A sample of 9.0 g of glucose is completely acetylated with acetic anhydride. Assuming 100% conversion, what mass of glucose pentaacetate is obtained? (C = 12, H = 1, O = 16)","image":"","options":["11.1 g","15.0 g","19.5 g","21.6 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 9.0 g glucose; molar mass of glucose = 180 g/mol, so moles = 0.05 mol.\nEach –OH becomes –OCOCH$_3$. This replaces one H with $COCH_3$, adding 42 g/mol per –OH group.\nPentaacetate: 180 + 5 × 42 = 390 g/mol, which is $C_{16}H_{22}O_{11}$.\nMass = 0.05 × 390 = 19.5 g.\n11.1 g would assume only one –OH group is acetylated.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (vitamin) with List-II (deficiency disease):\nList-I\n(a) Vitamin A\n(b) Vitamin B$_1$\n(c) Vitamin C\n(d) Vitamin K\nList-II\n(i) Scurvy\n(ii) Increased blood clotting time\n(iii) Night blindness\n(iv) Beri beri","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Vitamin A deficiency causes xerophthalmia and night blindness, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Vitamin B$_1$ (thiamine) deficiency causes beri beri, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) deficiency causes scurvy, with bleeding gums, so (c)-(i).\n(d) Vitamin K deficiency increases blood clotting time, so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Carbohydrates that give two to ten monosaccharide units on hydrolysis are classified as:","image":"","options":["Polysaccharides","Monosaccharides","Non-sugars","Oligosaccharides"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Carbohydrates are classified by their behaviour on hydrolysis.\nOligosaccharides yield 2–10 monosaccharide units. Examples are disaccharides and trisaccharides.\nPolysaccharides yield a large number of units.\nMonosaccharides cannot be hydrolysed further at all.\n'Non-sugars' is another name for polysaccharides, which are not sweet.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following vitamins is water soluble and yet can be stored in the human body?","image":"","options":["Vitamin C","Vitamin $B_{12}$","Vitamin $B_1$","Vitamin K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: B group vitamins and vitamin C are water soluble. They are readily excreted in urine and so must be supplied regularly in the diet.\nThe only exception that can be stored in the body is vitamin $B_{12}$.\nVitamin C and vitamin $B_1$ are water soluble but are not stored.\nVitamin K can be stored, but it is fat soluble (stored in liver and adipose tissue), so it does not satisfy both conditions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A sample of 3.42 g of sucrose is completely hydrolysed by boiling with dilute HCl in alcoholic solution. What mass of glucose is formed? (C = 12, H = 1, O = 16)","image":"","options":["0.90 g","1.71 g","1.80 g","3.60 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: mass of sucrose = 3.42 g; molar mass of $C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}$ = 342 g/mol.\nReaction: $C_{12}H_{22}O_{11} + H_2O \\rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6$ (glucose) + $C_6H_{12}O_6$ (fructose). One mole of sucrose gives one mole of glucose.\nMoles of sucrose = 3.42/342 = 0.01 mol, so moles of glucose = 0.01 mol.\nMass of glucose = 0.01 × 180 = 1.80 g.\n3.60 g would wrongly count the fructose as glucose.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): D-glucose is dextrorotatory whereas D-fructose is laevorotatory.\nReason (R): The prefix D indicates that a compound rotates plane-polarised light to the right.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Glucose is written as D-(+)-glucose, which is dextrorotatory. Fructose is written as D-(–)-fructose, which is laevorotatory.\nR is false. The letters D and L show relative configuration with respect to glyceraldehyde. They have no relation with optical activity.\nThe sign of rotation is shown separately by (+) or (–). This is why two D-sugars can rotate light in opposite directions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\n(i) Sucrose is dextrorotatory.\n(ii) Hydrolysis of sucrose gives an equimolar mixture of D-(+)-glucose and D-(–)-fructose.\n(iii) The hydrolysed mixture is laevorotatory because the magnitude of rotation of fructose ($-92.4^\\circ$) exceeds that of glucose ($+52.5^\\circ$).\n(iv) The letter 'D' in D-(–)-fructose shows that fructose rotates plane polarised light to the right.\n(v) Fructose forms a six-membered pyranose ring by addition of the C5–OH to the C2 keto group.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["4","2","5","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Sucrose itself is dextrorotatory.\n(ii) Correct. Sucrose hydrolyses to equal moles of glucose and fructose.\n(iii) Correct. The stronger laevorotation of fructose makes the mixture laevo, so it is called invert sugar.\n(iv) Incorrect. 'D' denotes relative configuration with respect to glyceraldehyde, not the direction of rotation. Fructose is in fact laevorotatory.\n(v) Incorrect. The C5–OH attacking C2 gives a five-membered furanose ring.\nCorrect statements = 3.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Based on the relative number of amino and carboxyl groups, which pair consists only of acidic amino acids?","image":"","options":["Lysine and arginine","Glutamine and asparagine","Aspartic acid and glutamic acid","Serine and threonine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An amino acid is acidic when it has more –COOH groups than –NH$_2$ groups.\nAspartic acid ($HOOC-CH_2-$) and glutamic acid ($HOOC-CH_2-CH_2-$) both have an extra carboxyl group in the side chain, so they are acidic.\nGlutamine and asparagine are the tempting trap. Their side chains are amides ($-CONH_2$), not acids, so they are not acidic.\nLysine and arginine are basic. Serine and threonine are neutral.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (reaction of glucose) with List-II (structural inference).\nList-I:\n(a) Prolonged heating with HI\n(b) Reaction with $NH_2OH$\n(c) Oxidation with bromine water\n(d) Oxidation with $HNO_3$\nList-II:\n(p) Carbonyl group is present\n(q) A primary alcoholic group is present\n(r) Six carbons are in a straight chain\n(s) Carbonyl group is aldehydic","image":"","options":["a-r, b-p, c-s, d-q","a-r, b-s, c-p, d-q","a-q, b-p, c-s, d-r","a-r, b-p, c-q, d-s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) HI gives n-hexane, so the six carbons form a straight chain (r).\n(b) Oxime formation shows that a carbonyl group (>C=O) is present (p).\n(c) Bromine water gives gluconic acid, so the carbonyl group is aldehydic (s).\n(d) $HNO_3$ gives the dicarboxylic saccharic acid, so a primary –OH group is present (q).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(P) Melting point of α-D-glucose\n(Q) Melting point of β-D-glucose\n(R) Temperature of crystallisation from concentrated solution giving the α-form\n(S) Temperature of crystallisation from hot saturated aqueous solution giving the β-form\nList-II\n(i) 423 K\n(ii) 303 K\n(iii) 419 K\n(iv) 371 K","image":"","options":["P-(iii), Q-(i), R-(ii), S-(iv)","P-(i), Q-(iii), R-(ii), S-(iv)","P-(iii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(ii)","P-(i), Q-(iii), R-(iv), S-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P: The α-form melts at 419 K, so P matches (iii).\nQ: The β-form melts at 423 K, so Q matches (i).\nR: The α-form crystallises from a concentrated solution at 303 K, so R matches (ii).\nS: The β-form crystallises from a hot saturated solution at 371 K, so S matches (iv).\nNote that the β-form has the higher melting point and also crystallises at the higher temperature.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"In disaccharides, two monosaccharide units are joined by a glycosidic linkage. Which description of this linkage is correct?","image":"","options":["An oxide linkage through an oxygen atom, formed with the loss of a water molecule","An amide (–CO–NH–) linkage formed with the loss of a water molecule","A direct carbon–carbon bond formed with the loss of hydrogen","A phosphodiester linkage between the 3′ and 5′ carbon atoms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines a glycosidic linkage as a link between two monosaccharide units through an oxygen atom.\nIt is an oxide linkage, and a water molecule is lost when it forms.\nThe –CO–NH– linkage is the peptide bond found in proteins.\nThe phosphodiester linkage joins nucleotides in nucleic acids.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Sucrose does not reduce Tollens' reagent, although both monosaccharides obtained from its hydrolysis are reducing sugars.\nStatement II: In sucrose, the glycosidic linkage joins C1 of $\\alpha$-D-glucose with C2 of $\\beta$-D-fructose, so neither the aldehydic nor the ketonic group is free.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Sucrose is non-reducing, but its hydrolysis products, glucose and fructose, are monosaccharides. All monosaccharides are reducing sugars.\nStatement II is correct. The C1–C2 glycosidic bond involves the aldehydic carbon of glucose (C1) and the ketonic carbon of fructose (C2).\nBecause no reducing group is left free, sucrose cannot reduce Fehling's solution or Tollens' reagent. Statement II thus accounts for Statement I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which nitrogenous base is found in RNA but not in DNA?","image":"","options":["Thymine","Cytosine","Uracil","Guanine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"DNA contains adenine, guanine, cytosine and thymine.\nRNA contains the same first three bases, but its fourth base is uracil instead of thymine.\nThymine is the base present only in DNA, which is the opposite of what is asked.\nCytosine and guanine occur in both DNA and RNA.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"The pentose sugar present in DNA is","image":"","options":["$\\beta$-D-ribose","$\\beta$-D-2-deoxyribose","$\\alpha$-D-glucose","$\\beta$-D-fructose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Complete hydrolysis of a nucleic acid gives a pentose sugar, phosphoric acid and nitrogenous bases.\nIn DNA the sugar is $\\beta$-D-2-deoxyribose. It has no –OH at C-2′.\nIn RNA the sugar is $\\beta$-D-ribose, so option (1) describes RNA, not DNA.\nGlucose and fructose are hexoses and are not nucleic acid sugars.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following amino acids are essential amino acids (must be obtained through diet)?\nValine, glycine, lysine, serine, tryptophan, histidine, proline, threonine","image":"","options":["3","4","6","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Essential amino acids cannot be synthesised by the body and must come from the diet.\nAccording to NCERT Table 10.2, valine, lysine, tryptophan, histidine and threonine are essential. That makes 5.\nGlycine, serine and proline are non-essential because the body can synthesise them.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Glucose reacts with hydroxylamine to form an oxime.\nReason (R): The formation of an oxime proves that the carbonyl group in glucose is an aldehyde group.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Glucose forms an oxime with $NH_2OH$, which shows that a >C=O group is present.\nR is false. Both aldehydes and ketones form oximes, so this reaction cannot tell them apart.\nThe aldehydic nature is shown by oxidation with bromine water to gluconic acid, which keeps all six carbons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Most vitamins cannot be synthesised in the human body, while plants can synthesise almost all of them; hence vitamins are regarded as essential food factors.\nStatement II: The bacteria present in the human gut cannot produce any of the vitamins required by us.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: correct. NCERT states that most vitamins are not synthesised in our body but plants make almost all of them. This is why vitamins are treated as essential food factors.\nStatement II: incorrect. NCERT notes that gut bacteria can produce some of the vitamins we need.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A sample of sucrose is completely hydrolysed to invert sugar. The specific rotation of D-(+)-glucose is $+52.5^\\circ$ and that of D-(–)-fructose is $-92.4^\\circ$. Both have the formula C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$. Assume the specific rotation of the mixture is the mass-weighted average of its components. What is the specific rotation of invert sugar?","image":"","options":["$-39.9^\\circ$","$-19.95^\\circ$","$+19.95^\\circ$","$-92.4^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: hydrolysis of sucrose gives an equimolar mixture of glucose and fructose. Both have the same molar mass, so their masses are also equal (mass fraction 1/2 each).\nFormula: $[\\alpha]_{mix} = \\frac{1}{2}[\\alpha]_{glu} + \\frac{1}{2}[\\alpha]_{fru}$\nSubstituting: $[\\alpha]_{mix} = \\frac{52.5 + (-92.4)}{2} = \\frac{-39.9}{2}$\nSo the specific rotation of invert sugar is $-19.95^\\circ$. It is laevorotatory, which is why the sign of rotation inverts on hydrolysis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Thyroxine, produced in the thyroid gland, is an iodinated derivative of the amino acid tyrosine.\nStatement II: Insulin and glucagon both act to lower the blood glucose level.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT describes thyroxine as an iodinated derivative of tyrosine. Low iodine in the diet can lead to hypothyroidism.\nStatement II is incorrect. Insulin is released when blood glucose rises sharply and lowers it. Glucagon tends to increase blood glucose.\nTogether, these two opposing hormones keep glucose within narrow limits.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Cellulose is the most abundant organic substance in the plant kingdom and is a predominant constituent of plant cell walls.\nStatement II: Cellulose is a branched polysaccharide in which branching occurs through C1–C6 glycosidic linkages.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Cellulose occurs only in plants, is the most abundant organic substance in the plant kingdom, and is the main constituent of plant cell walls.\nStatement II is incorrect. Cellulose is a straight-chain polymer of β-D-glucose with only C1–C4 links.\nC1–C6 branching is a feature of amylopectin and glycogen.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"In the name D-(+)-glucose, what does the symbol (+) indicate?","image":"","options":["The –OH on the lowest asymmetric carbon is on the right side","Glucose rotates plane-polarised light to the right (dextrorotatory)","Glucose is chemically correlated to D-glyceraldehyde","Glucose exists as the β-anomer"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In D-(+)-glucose, 'D' gives the configuration and '(+)' gives the direction of optical rotation.\n(+) means the compound is dextrorotatory: it turns plane-polarised light to the right.\nOptions (1) and (3) describe the meaning of 'D', not '(+)'.\nOption (4) is wrong because (+) says nothing about anomers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A glycosidic linkage between two monosaccharide units is formed with the loss of a water molecule.\n(ii) Sucrose itself is dextrorotatory.\n(iii) Hydrolysis of maltose gives glucose and galactose.\n(iv) In lactose, the free aldehyde group can be generated at C1 of the galactose unit.\n(v) The hydrolysed mixture of sucrose is laevorotatory because the laevorotation of fructose is larger than the dextrorotation of glucose.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. The oxide (glycosidic) bridge forms by elimination of $H_2O$.\n(ii) Correct. Sucrose is dextrorotatory before hydrolysis.\n(iii) Incorrect. Maltose gives only glucose (two molecules).\n(iv) Incorrect. Galactose C1 is used in the glycosidic bond, so the free aldehyde forms at C1 of the glucose unit.\n(v) Correct. Fructose has a rotation of –92.4°, which outweighs +52.5° for glucose.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"In a sample of double-stranded DNA, adenine makes up 18% of the total bases. Assuming complementary pairing as in the Watson–Crick model, what is the percentage of guanine?","image":"","options":["18%","36%","32%","64%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: A = 18%.\nRule: A pairs with T, so T = A = 18%. G pairs with C, so G = C.\nSubstituting: A + T = 36%, so G + C = 100 − 36 = 64%.\nSo G = 64/2 = 32%.\nOption (4), 64%, is G + C together, not guanine alone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"The term 'vitamine' was coined from 'vital + amine'. Why was the final letter 'e' later dropped to give the present word 'vitamin'?","image":"","options":["Vitamins were found not to be vital for life","Most vitamins were later found not to contain amino groups","Vitamins were found to be proteins rather than amines","All vitamins were found to be soluble in fats"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The first vitamins identified contained amino groups, so the name 'vitamine' (vital + amine) was used.\nLater work showed that most vitamins do not contain amino groups.\nThe 'e' linked to 'amine' was therefore dropped.\nThey are still considered vital, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements correctly describes how nucleotides are joined in a nucleic acid chain?","image":"","options":["The base is attached at the 5′ carbon of the sugar, and the phosphate links the 1′ carbons of adjacent sugars","A phosphate group links the 3′ carbon of one sugar to the 5′ carbon of the next sugar","Adjacent nucleotides are joined by peptide linkages between their bases","The primed numbers (1′, 2′, 3′…) are used to number the atoms of the bases, not of the sugar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the base sits at the 1′ position and the phosphate at the 5′ position, not the reverse.\n(2) Correct: nucleotides are joined by a phosphodiester linkage between the 5′ and 3′ carbons of the pentose sugars. This builds the sugar–phosphate backbone.\n(3) Incorrect: peptide bonds belong to proteins. Bases form hydrogen bonds between strands, not covalent chain links.\n(4) Incorrect: primes are used for the sugar carbons, to distinguish them from the base atoms.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are reducing sugars (i.e., reduce Fehling's solution and Tollens' reagent)?\nGlucose, fructose, sucrose, maltose, lactose, ribose","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"All monosaccharides, whether aldose or ketose, are reducing sugars. So glucose, fructose and ribose all reduce these reagents.\nMaltose and lactose can generate a free aldehyde group at C1 of a glucose unit, so they are reducing sugars.\nIn sucrose, the reducing groups of both glucose and fructose are tied up in the glycosidic bond, so it is non-reducing.\nCount of reducing sugars = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"In which protein do the polypeptide chains run parallel and are held together by hydrogen and disulphide bonds, giving a water-insoluble, fibre-like structure?","image":"","options":["Insulin","Albumin","Keratin","Maltase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fibrous proteins have parallel polypeptide chains held by hydrogen and disulphide bonds. They are generally insoluble in water.\nNCERT gives keratin (in hair, wool and silk) and myosin (in muscles) as examples.\nInsulin and albumins are globular proteins. Their chains coil into a spherical shape, and they are usually water-soluble.\nMaltase is an enzyme. Almost all enzymes are globular proteins.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Open-chain D-glucose cyclises to its six-membered pyranose form. How does the number of asymmetric (chiral) carbon atoms change, and which carbon is newly made chiral?","image":"","options":["From 4 to 4; no new chiral carbon is formed","From 4 to 5; C-1 becomes chiral","From 5 to 6; C-6 becomes chiral","From 4 to 5; C-5 becomes chiral"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Open chain: C-2, C-3, C-4 and C-5 are asymmetric, which makes 4. C-1 (–CHO) and C-6 (–CH$_2$OH) are not.\nCyclisation: the C-5 –OH adds to the C-1 carbonyl to form a hemiacetal.\nC-1 now carries four different groups: H, OH, the ring O and C-2. So C-1 becomes a new chiral centre, called the anomeric carbon. The total becomes 5.\nThe two possible arrangements at C-1 give the α- and β-anomers.\nOption (4) is wrong because C-5 was already chiral.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following naturally occurring α-amino acids is optically inactive?","image":"","options":["Alanine","Glycine","Serine","Valine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An α-amino acid is optically active only when its α-carbon is asymmetric, that is, bonded to four different groups.\nIn glycine the side chain R is H. The α-carbon therefore carries two H atoms and is not asymmetric.\nAlanine ($-CH_3$), serine ($-CH_2OH$) and valine ($-CH(CH_3)_2$) each have an asymmetric α-carbon, so they are optically active.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"The dipeptide glycylalanine is formed when the carboxyl group of glycine combines with the amino group of alanine. In glycylalanine, the free –NH$_2$ group and the free –COOH group belong respectively to","image":"","options":["alanine and glycine","glycine and alanine","glycine and glycine","alanine and alanine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Glycine's –COOH reacts with alanine's –NH$_2$ to form the –CO–NH– bond, with loss of water.\nThe groups left unreacted are glycine's –NH$_2$ and alanine's –COOH.\nSo the free –NH$_2$ belongs to glycine and the free –COOH belongs to alanine.\nOption (1) reverses this, which would describe alanylglycine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the cyclic structures of D-glucose is correct?","image":"","options":["The pyranose ring contains six carbon atoms, with C-6 forming part of the ring","Cyclisation makes C-1 a new asymmetric centre, so cyclic glucose has five asymmetric carbons whereas open-chain glucose has four","The α and β forms differ in configuration at C-5, which is the carbon whose –OH forms the ring","Since the α and β forms have different specific rotations, one belongs to the D-series and the other to the L-series"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect. A pyranose ring has five carbons (C-1 to C-5) and one oxygen. C-6 (–CH$_2$OH) stays outside the ring.\n(2) Correct. In the open chain, C-2 to C-5 are asymmetric. When the C-5 –OH adds to C-1, C-1 carries four different groups and becomes asymmetric. This gives five asymmetric carbons and the two anomers.\n(3) Incorrect. Anomers differ only at C-1, the anomeric carbon.\n(4) Incorrect. Both forms have the same C-5 configuration, so both are D. Rotation does not decide D or L.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following hormones tends to raise the glucose level in the blood?","image":"","options":["Insulin","Progesterone","Glucagon","Estradiol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Insulin is released when blood glucose rises rapidly, and it brings the glucose level down.\nGlucagon acts in the opposite way and tends to increase the blood glucose level.\nTogether, these two hormones keep blood glucose within narrow limits.\nProgesterone and estradiol are female sex hormones and do not regulate blood glucose.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"In the structure of nucleic acids, a nucleoside is converted into a nucleotide when phosphoric acid becomes linked to which position of the pentose sugar?","image":"","options":["1′-position","2′-position","3′-position","5′-position"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A nucleoside forms when a base attaches to the 1′ carbon of the pentose sugar.\nA nucleotide forms when this nucleoside links to phosphoric acid at the 5′ position of the sugar.\n1′ is where the base attaches, not the phosphate.\n2′ is the carbon that differs between ribose and 2-deoxyribose.\nThe 3′ carbon takes part in the phosphodiester link to the next nucleotide. It is not the site of the nucleotide's own phosphate.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair of polysaccharides has glucose units joined by the same type of glycosidic linkage positions (C1–C4 only, with no branching), but differs in the anomeric form of glucose used?","image":"","options":["Amylopectin and glycogen","Amylose and cellulose","Amylose and amylopectin","Cellulose and glycogen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Amylose is an unbranched chain of α-D-glucose units with C1–C4 links only.\nCellulose is a straight chain of β-D-glucose units, also with C1–C4 links only.\nSo these two differ only in the anomer of glucose: α in amylose and β in cellulose.\nAmylopectin and glycogen are both α-glucose polymers with C1–C6 branches, so they fail the 'no branching' condition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"18 g of glucose is completely acetylated with excess acetic anhydride to give glucose pentaacetate. What is the mass of pentaacetate formed? (Atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1, O = 16 u; assume 100% yield)","image":"","options":["22.2 g","34.8 g","39.0 g","39.5 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: glucose $C_6H_{12}O_6$, M = 180 g/mol, so 18 g = 0.1 mol.\nFormula: each acetylation replaces the H of one –OH by $CH_3CO$. This adds $C_2H_2O$ = 42 g/mol per –OH.\nSubstituting: M(pentaacetate) = 180 + 5 × 42 = 390 g/mol.\nMass formed = 0.1 × 390 = 39.0 g.\nThe tempting 39.5 g comes from adding the full acetyl mass (43) without removing the H.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Sodium iodide is added to commercial table salt to make 'iodised salt'. This practice mainly helps to control:","image":"","options":["Hyperthyroidism caused by excess thyroxine","Hypothyroidism and enlargement of the thyroid gland","Addison's disease","Pernicious anaemia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A low level of iodine in the diet reduces thyroxine production. This leads to hypothyroidism and enlargement of the thyroid gland.\nAdding sodium iodide to table salt supplies the iodine needed and keeps this condition under control.\nHyperthyroidism is caused by too much thyroxine, so extra iodine would not prevent it.\nAddison's disease involves the adrenal cortex.\nPernicious anaemia is a vitamin $B_{12}$ deficiency.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Insulin and glucagon together keep the blood glucose level within narrow limits.\nReason (R): Insulin is released in response to a rapid fall in blood glucose level.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Insulin lowers blood glucose and glucagon raises it, so together they regulate glucose within a narrow range.\nR is false. Insulin is released in response to a rapid rise in blood glucose, not a fall.\nA low glucose level is countered by glucagon instead.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Curdling of milk, caused by lactic acid formed by bacteria present in milk, is an example of denaturation of protein.\nStatement II: During denaturation, the primary structure of the protein is destroyed along with its secondary and tertiary structures.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Lactic acid lowers the pH, which disturbs the hydrogen bonds and denatures milk proteins. NCERT cites this as an example of denaturation.\nStatement II is incorrect. Denaturation destroys the secondary and tertiary structures, but the primary structure (the amino acid sequence) remains intact.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A sample of double-stranded DNA contains 30% adenine (by number of bases). What percentage of its bases is cytosine?","image":"","options":["20%","30%","40%","70%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 30%. In double-stranded DNA, adenine pairs only with thymine and cytosine pairs only with guanine.\nFormula: A = T and C = G, with A + T + G + C = 100%.\nSubstituting: T = 30%, so G + C = 100 − 60 = 40%.\nSo C = 40/2 = 20%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which set of vitamins is stored in the liver and adipose (fat-storing) tissues of the body?","image":"","options":["B1, B2, C and K","A, D, E and K","A, C, D and E","B12, C, D and K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fat-soluble vitamins dissolve in fats and oils but not in water. These are A, D, E and K.\nThey are stored in the liver and adipose tissues.\nB group vitamins and vitamin C are water-soluble and are readily excreted in urine.\nEvery other option contains at least one water-soluble vitamin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following carbohydrates will reduce Tollens' reagent?\nGlucose, fructose, ribose, galactose, sucrose, maltose, lactose","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Monosaccharides: NCERT states that all monosaccharides, whether aldoses or ketoses, are reducing. Glucose, fructose, ribose and galactose are all reducing, which gives 4.\nMaltose: a free aldehyde group can form at C-1 of the second glucose unit, so it is reducing. That makes 5.\nLactose: a free aldehyde group can form at C-1 of the glucose unit, so it is reducing. That makes 6.\nSucrose: C-1 of glucose and C-2 of fructose are both used in the glycosidic bond, so it is non-reducing.\nTotal = 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (amino acid) with List-II (one-letter code).\nList-I:\n(a) Lysine\n(b) Glutamic acid\n(c) Tryptophan\n(d) Asparagine\nList-II:\n(i) W\n(ii) N\n(iii) K\n(iv) E","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The codes come from Table 10.2.\n(a) Lysine (Lys) has the code K, because L is already used by leucine.\n(b) Glutamic acid (Glu) has the code E, because G is used by glycine.\n(c) Tryptophan (Trp) has the code W.\n(d) Asparagine (Asn) has the code N, because A is used by alanine.\nThe correct matching is (a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about amino acids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In a β-amino acid, the amino group is on the second carbon counted away from the carboxyl carbon, whereas in an α-amino acid it is on the carbon adjacent to –COOH","Only α-amino acids are obtained on hydrolysis of proteins","Since most naturally occurring amino acids have L-configuration, all of them are laevorotatory","Amino acids in zwitter ionic form react with both acids and bases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. α means the amino group is on the carbon next to –COOH, and β means it is on the following carbon.\n(2) Correct. Proteins are polymers of α-amino acids only.\n(3) Incorrect. D and L describe configuration relative to glyceraldehyde. They have no fixed relation to the sign of optical rotation, so an L-amino acid may be dextro- or laevorotatory.\n(4) Correct. This is the amphoteric nature of amino acids.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A monosaccharide contains seven carbon atoms and a free aldehyde group in its open-chain form. Its correct general name is:","image":"","options":["Ketoheptose","Aldohexose","Aldoheptose","Aldopentose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The prefix 'aldo' indicates an aldehyde group, and 'keto' indicates a ketone group.\nThe carbon count gives the stem: triose (3), tetrose (4), pentose (5), hexose (6), heptose (7).\nAn aldehyde group with 7 C atoms therefore gives aldoheptose.\nKetoheptose would need a keto group. Aldohexose has only 6 C atoms.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (carbohydrate) with List-II (glycosidic linkage present):\nList-I\n(A) Sucrose\n(B) Maltose\n(C) Lactose\n(D) Branch point of amylopectin\nList-II\n(i) C1 of $\\beta$-D-galactose to C4 of $\\beta$-D-glucose\n(ii) C1 of $\\alpha$-D-glucose to C6 of $\\alpha$-D-glucose\n(iii) C1 of $\\alpha$-D-glucose to C2 of $\\beta$-D-fructose\n(iv) C1 of $\\alpha$-D-glucose to C4 of $\\alpha$-D-glucose","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Sucrose: C1 of $\\alpha$-D-glucose is linked to C2 of $\\beta$-D-fructose, so (iii).\n(B) Maltose: two $\\alpha$-D-glucose units are joined C1 to C4, so (iv).\n(C) Lactose: C1 of $\\beta$-D-galactose is linked to C4 of $\\beta$-D-glucose, so (i).\n(D) Amylopectin: the chain uses C1–C4 links, but branching occurs through C1–C6 links, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Nucleoside\n(b) Nucleotide\n(c) Phosphodiester linkage\n(d) Primary structure of nucleic acid\nList-II\n(i) Sequence of nucleotides in the chain\n(ii) Base + sugar + phosphate\n(iii) Base attached to 1′ carbon of sugar\n(iv) Joins 5′ and 3′ carbons of pentose sugars of adjacent nucleotides","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) A nucleoside is a base attached at the 1′ position of the sugar → (iii).\n(b) A nucleotide is a nucleoside plus phosphate at 5′, i.e. base + sugar + phosphate → (ii).\n(c) A phosphodiester linkage joins nucleotides between the 5′ and 3′ carbons of the sugars → (iv).\n(d) The primary structure is the sequence of nucleotides → (i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the β-pleated sheet, polypeptide chains are stretched to nearly maximum extension and held side by side by intermolecular hydrogen bonds, whereas in the α-helix the hydrogen bonds link groups on adjacent turns of the same chain.\nStatement II: Since the 2° and 3° structures are stabilised by hydrogen bonds, disulphide linkages, van der Waals and electrostatic forces, denaturation must break the peptide bonds of the protein.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In the β-pleated sheet, stretched chains are joined by intermolecular H-bonds. In the α-helix, the –NH of a residue bonds to a C=O of the adjacent turn within the same chain.\nStatement II lists the stabilising forces correctly, but its conclusion is wrong.\nDenaturation only disturbs those forces, mainly the H-bonds. The peptide bonds stay intact, so the primary structure survives.\nTherefore Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Complete hydrolysis of a sample of RNA is carried out. How many of the following would be obtained among the products?\nβ-D-ribose, thymine, uracil, adenine, β-D-2-deoxyribose, cytosine, phosphoric acid","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Complete hydrolysis of RNA gives three kinds of product: the pentose sugar, phosphoric acid and the bases.\nβ-D-ribose: obtained, since it is the sugar of RNA.\nThymine: not obtained, as it occurs only in DNA.\nUracil: obtained.\nAdenine: obtained.\nβ-D-2-deoxyribose: not obtained, as it is the sugar of DNA.\nCytosine: obtained.\nPhosphoric acid: obtained.\nThe total is 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes are examples of protein denaturation?\n(i) Coagulation of egg white on boiling\n(ii) Curdling of milk by lactic acid formed by bacteria\n(iii) Complete acid hydrolysis of a protein into its α-amino acids\n(iv) Uncoiling of the α-helix of a protein on heating\n(v) Formation of a dipeptide from two amino acids","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: this is the standard NCERT example of denaturation.\n(ii) Yes: the change in pH from lactic acid denatures the milk proteins.\n(iii) No: hydrolysis breaks the peptide bonds and destroys the primary structure, while denaturation keeps it intact.\n(iv) Yes: heat disturbs the hydrogen bonds, so the helix uncoils.\n(v) No: this is peptide bond formation.\nThat makes 3 examples.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"In the dipeptide glycylalanine, formed as described in NCERT, which groups remain free (not involved in the peptide bond)?","image":"","options":["The –COOH group of glycine and the –NH$_2$ group of alanine","The –NH$_2$ group of glycine and the –COOH group of alanine","Both –NH$_2$ groups; the two –COOH groups combine","The –NH$_2$ and –COOH groups of glycine only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In glycylalanine, the carboxyl group of glycine combines with the amino group of alanine. This releases water and forms –CO–NH–.\nThe groups not used in this bond are the –NH$_2$ of glycine and the –COOH of alanine, so these remain free.\nOption (1) describes the reverse arrangement, which is alanylglycine.\nTwo –COOH groups cannot form a peptide bond with each other.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Glycine is the only naturally occurring $\\alpha$-amino acid that is optically inactive.\nStatement II: Most naturally occurring $\\alpha$-amino acids have D-configuration.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Glycine has R = H, so its $\\alpha$-carbon carries two hydrogens and is not asymmetric. All other natural $\\alpha$-amino acids are optically active.\nStatement II is incorrect. Most naturally occurring amino acids have L-configuration, written with –NH$_2$ on the left-hand side.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Sucrose does not reduce Tollens' reagent or Fehling's solution.\nReason (R): In sucrose, the glycosidic linkage joins C1 of $\\alpha$-D-glucose and C2 of $\\beta$-D-fructose, so the reducing groups of both monosaccharides are tied up in the bond.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar, so it does not reduce Tollens' reagent or Fehling's solution.\nR is true. The C1 aldehydic carbon of glucose and the C2 ketonic carbon of fructose form the glycosidic bond.\nNo free aldehyde or keto group can be regenerated in solution. This is exactly why sucrose is non-reducing, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Chemically, almost all enzymes are:","image":"","options":["Fibrous proteins","Globular proteins","Polysaccharides","Nucleic acids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that almost all enzymes are globular proteins that act as biocatalysts.\nGlobular proteins have polypeptide chains coiled into a spherical shape, and they are usually water-soluble.\nFibrous proteins such as keratin and myosin are water-insoluble structural proteins, not enzymes.\nPolysaccharides and nucleic acids are not the chemical class of enzymes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Vitamin) with List-II (Deficiency disease).\nList-I:\n(A) Vitamin $B_1$\n(B) Vitamin $B_{12}$\n(C) Vitamin D\n(D) Vitamin K\nList-II:\n(I) Increased blood clotting time\n(II) Rickets\n(III) Beri beri\n(IV) Pernicious anaemia","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Thiamine ($B_1$) deficiency causes beri beri: A-III.\n(B) $B_{12}$ deficiency causes pernicious anaemia, in which RBCs are deficient in haemoglobin: B-IV.\n(C) Vitamin D deficiency causes rickets in children: C-II.\n(D) Vitamin K deficiency increases blood clotting time: D-I.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Imagine a sugar made from D-glucose by inverting only the configuration at C-5 in its Fischer projection, keeping C-2, C-3 and C-4 unchanged. Which description of this sugar is correct?","image":"","options":["It is L-glucose, the enantiomer of D-glucose","It is still a D-sugar, because three of its four asymmetric carbons are unchanged","It is D-fructose","It belongs to the L-series but is not L-glucose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The D/L series of a monosaccharide is decided only by the lowest asymmetric carbon (C-5 in glucose). If its –OH moves to the left, the sugar belongs to the L-series.\nAn enantiomer such as L-glucose needs every asymmetric centre inverted. Here C-2 to C-4 are unchanged, so this is a different L-aldohexose, not L-glucose.\nIt is still an aldose, so it cannot be fructose, which is a ketose.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Insulin, which contains only 51 amino acid residues, is usually called a protein.\nReason (R): Every polypeptide containing more than ten amino acid residues is called a protein.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The polypeptide/protein distinction is not sharp. Insulin is called a protein because it has a well-defined protein conformation.\nR is false. A chain of more than ten amino acids is only called a polypeptide.\nThe term 'protein' is normally kept for chains of more than 100 residues with molecular mass above 10,000 u.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following conclusions drawn from the reactions of glucose is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Formation of n-hexane on prolonged heating with HI shows that the six carbons are in a straight chain","Oxidation by bromine water to gluconic acid shows that the carbonyl group is aldehydic","Formation of a stable pentaacetate shows five –OH groups attached to different carbon atoms","Oxidation by nitric acid to saccharic acid shows the presence of a secondary alcoholic group"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Option (1) is correct: forming n-hexane with HI indicates an unbranched six-carbon chain.\nOption (2) is correct: a mild oxidant converts –CHO to –COOH without losing a carbon, so the carbonyl is aldehydic.\nOption (3) is correct: a stable pentaacetate means five –OH groups sit on different carbons.\nOption (4) is incorrect: in saccharic acid the terminal –CH$_2$OH has also become –COOH, which proves a primary alcoholic group.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about amino acids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Hydrolysis of proteins gives only α-amino acids, in which the –$NH_2$ group is attached to the carbon next to the –COOH group","Lysine, with side chain $H_2N-(CH_2)_4-$, is a basic amino acid","Glutamine, with side chain $H_2N-CO-CH_2-CH_2-$, is an acidic amino acid because its side chain is derived from that of glutamic acid","Most naturally occurring amino acids have L-configuration, which is represented by writing the –$NH_2$ group on the left-hand side"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Proteins yield only α-amino acids, and in these the amino group sits on the α-carbon next to –COOH.\n(2) Correct. Lysine has two amino groups and one carboxyl group, so it is basic.\n(3) Incorrect. Glutamine's side chain ends in an amide (–CO–$NH_2$), not a free –COOH. It has one amino group and one carboxyl group, so it is neutral. Glutamic acid, with two –COOH groups, is the acidic one.\n(4) Correct. This is how NCERT describes natural amino acids and the L-convention.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Acid hydrolysis of sucrose produces a mixture called invert sugar.\nReason (R): The dextrorotation of glucose is greater in magnitude than the laevorotation of fructose.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sucrose is dextrorotatory, but its hydrolysis gives an equimolar mixture that is laevorotatory. Because the sign of rotation is inverted, the product is called invert sugar, so A is true.\nThe specific rotation of fructose is −92.4° and that of glucose is +52.5°. The laevorotation of fructose is the larger one, so R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A double-stranded DNA segment contains 1000 base pairs. Adenine makes up 28% of the total number of bases in the segment. Assuming strict Watson–Crick base pairing, how many guanine bases are present in the segment?","image":"","options":["440","560","220","880"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 1000 base pairs, so the total number of bases = 2000. Adenine = 28% of the total.\nRule: A pairs with T and G pairs with C. So %A = %T and %G = %C.\nSubstituting: %T = 28%, so %(G + C) = 100 − 56 = 44%. This gives %G = 22%.\nNumber of G = 0.22 × 2000 = 440.\nOption 220 is what you get if you use 1000 bases instead of 2000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following compounds fits the general formula $C_x(H_2O)_y$ but is NOT a carbohydrate?","image":"","options":["Glucose","Sucrose","Acetic acid","Rhamnose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Acetic acid, $CH_3COOH$ ($C_2H_4O_2$), can be written as $C_2(H_2O)_2$, so it fits the formula. It is not a polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone, so it is not a carbohydrate.\nGlucose ($C_6(H_2O)_6$) and sucrose ($C_{12}(H_2O)_{11}$) fit the formula and are carbohydrates.\nRhamnose ($C_6H_{12}O_5$) is a carbohydrate that does not fit the formula.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A linear double-stranded DNA fragment contains 120 base pairs. Each strand is a continuous linear polynucleotide chain. What is the total number of phosphodiester linkages present in the fragment?","image":"","options":["119","120","238","240"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 120 base pairs, so each strand has 120 nucleotides.\nRule: in a linear chain, each phosphodiester linkage joins two adjacent nucleotides. So n nucleotides need n − 1 linkages.\nSubstituting: one strand has 120 − 1 = 119 linkages.\nThe two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds, not phosphodiester bonds.\nSo the total is 2 × 119 = 238.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (vitamin) with List-II (deficiency disease).\nList-I:\n(A) Vitamin $B_2$\n(B) Vitamin $B_6$\n(C) Vitamin E\n(D) Vitamin D\nList-II:\n(I) Increased fragility of RBCs and muscular weakness\n(II) Osteomalacia in adults\n(III) Cheilosis (fissuring at corners of mouth and lips)\n(IV) Convulsions\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Vitamin $B_2$ (riboflavin): its deficiency causes cheilosis, digestive disorders and burning of the skin, so A-III.\n(B) Vitamin $B_6$ (pyridoxine): its deficiency causes convulsions, so B-IV.\n(C) Vitamin E: its deficiency causes increased fragility of RBCs and muscular weakness, so C-I.\n(D) Vitamin D: its deficiency causes rickets in children and osteomalacia (soft bones, joint pain) in adults, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following disaccharides does NOT reduce Fehling's solution or Tollens' reagent?","image":"","options":["Maltose","Lactose","Sucrose","Both maltose and lactose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In sucrose, C1 of α-D-glucose is bonded to C2 of β-D-fructose.\nBoth reducing groups (the aldehydic and the ketonic carbon) are used up in the glycosidic bond, so no free carbonyl group can form in solution. Sucrose is therefore non-reducing.\nMaltose and lactose can each produce a free aldehyde group at C1 of a glucose unit, so both are reducing sugars.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"In proteins, α-amino acids are joined to one another by a peptide linkage. Chemically, this linkage is","image":"","options":["an ester group, –CO–O–","an amide group, –CO–NH–","a glycosidic linkage, –O–","a phosphodiester linkage"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A peptide bond forms when the –COOH group of one amino acid combines with the –NH$_2$ group of another, and a water molecule is eliminated.\nThe linkage formed is –CO–NH–, which is an amide.\nAn ester (–CO–O–) would need an –OH group, not –NH$_2$, to react with the acid.\nGlycosidic linkages join monosaccharides, and phosphodiester linkages join nucleotides.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"The activation energy for hydrolysis of sucrose is 6.22 kJ $mol^{-1}$ with acid and 2.15 kJ $mol^{-1}$ with the enzyme sucrase. Assume the Arrhenius pre-exponential factor is the same in both cases, T = 298 K and R = 8.314 J $mol^{-1}$ $K^{-1}$. What is the ratio $\\frac{k_{enzyme}}{k_{acid}}$ of the rate constants?","image":"","options":["5.17","1.64","2.89","0.19"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta E_a$ = 6.22 − 2.15 = 4.07 kJ/mol = 4070 J/mol, with T = 298 K.\nFormula: $\\frac{k_{enz}}{k_{acid}} = e^{\\Delta E_a/RT}$\nSubstituting: the exponent is $\\frac{4070}{8.314 \\times 298} = 1.643$, so the ratio is $e^{1.643}$.\nSo the ratio is about 5.17.\nOption (2), 1.64, is only the exponent. Option (3), 2.89, is just the ratio of the two activation energies.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about enzymes are correct?\n(i) An enzyme is very specific for a particular reaction and for a particular substrate.\n(ii) The names of enzymes generally end in -ase.\n(iii) Enzymes are required in large amounts because they are consumed during the reaction.\n(iv) Enzymes speed up reactions by increasing the activation energy.\n(v) Enzymes may be named after their substrate or after the reaction they catalyse.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: enzymes are specific for both the reaction and the substrate.\n(ii) Correct: enzyme names typically end in -ase, e.g., maltase.\n(iii) Incorrect: enzymes are needed only in small quantities and are not consumed.\n(iv) Incorrect: enzymes lower the activation energy; they do not raise it.\n(v) Correct: maltase is named after its substrate, and oxidoreductase after its reaction.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The mixture obtained by complete hydrolysis of sucrose using the enzyme sucrase is laevorotatory.\nStatement II: Because sucrase lowers the activation energy from 6.22 kJ $mol^{-1}$ to 2.15 kJ $mol^{-1}$, enzymatic hydrolysis gives products different from those of acid hydrolysis.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: hydrolysis gives an equimolar mixture of D-(+)-glucose (+52.5°) and D-(−)-fructose (−92.4°).\nThe laevorotation of fructose is larger, so the mixture (invert sugar) is laevorotatory.\nStatement II is incorrect: an enzyme only lowers the energy barrier and speeds up the reaction.\nIt does not change the products, so both routes give glucose and fructose.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In the α-helix, the polypeptide chain twists into a right-handed screw.\n(ii) In the α-helix, the –NH group of each amino acid residue is hydrogen bonded to the C=O of an adjacent turn of the helix.\n(iii) In the β-pleated sheet, the peptide chains are stretched to nearly maximum extension and held side by side by intermolecular hydrogen bonds.\n(iv) Fibrous proteins such as myosin are generally soluble in water.\n(v) Every protein possesses a quaternary structure.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the α-helix is a right-handed screw.\n(ii) Correct: each –NH is hydrogen bonded to the C=O of an adjacent turn.\n(iii) Correct: in the β-pleated sheet, the chains are fully stretched and joined by intermolecular hydrogen bonds, like drapery folds.\n(iv) Incorrect: fibrous proteins are generally insoluble in water.\n(v) Incorrect: only proteins made of two or more sub-units, such as haemoglobin, have a quaternary structure.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"D-ribose and 2-deoxy-D-ribose occur in nucleic acids. Both of these sugars are classified as","image":"","options":["Aldopentoses","Ketopentoses","Aldohexoses","Ketohexoses"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT names D-ribose and 2-deoxy-D-ribose as two aldopentoses present in nucleic acids.\nEach has five carbon atoms (pentose) and an aldehyde group (aldose).\nThey are not ketoses. Fructose is the familiar ketose, and it is a hexose.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a nucleotide, phosphoric acid is attached at the 5′ position of the sugar, and successive nucleotides in a nucleic acid chain are joined by phosphodiester linkages between the 5′ and 3′ carbons of the pentose sugar.\nStatement II: Because adenine pairs with uracil and guanine pairs with cytosine, hydrolysis of any RNA always gives equal amounts of adenine and uracil.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A base at C1′ plus sugar gives a nucleoside. Phosphate at C5′ makes it a nucleotide. Nucleotides are joined by 5′–3′ phosphodiester links.\nStatement II is incorrect. Equal base amounts arise only from complementary pairing between two strands, as A–T and G–C in double-stranded DNA.\nRNA is a single strand, which sometimes folds back on itself. Hence its hydrolysis shows no fixed relationship among base quantities.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The letters D and L tell us the direction in which a compound rotates plane-polarised light.\n(ii) D and L mean the same as the prefixes d and l.\n(iii) In D-(+)-glyceraldehyde, the –OH group on the asymmetric carbon lies on the right-hand side.\n(iv) When D/L is assigned, the structure is written with the most oxidised carbon at the top.\n(v) The cyclic forms of fructose are formed when the –OH at C-5 adds to the C-2 keto group, giving a five-membered furanose ring.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: D and L have no relation to optical activity.\n(ii) Incorrect: D/L are not related to d/l.\n(iii) Correct: in the D-isomer of glyceraldehyde, the –OH is on the right.\n(iv) Correct: the –CHO (most oxidised carbon) is written at the top.\n(v) Correct: this is how the furanose ring of fructose forms.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Polysaccharide) with List-II (Structural feature).\nList-I:\n(A) Amylose\n(B) Amylopectin\n(C) Cellulose\n(D) Glycogen\nList-II:\n(I) Straight chain of $\\beta$-D-glucose units joined by C1–C4 linkage\n(II) Unbranched chain of $\\alpha$-D-glucose units; water soluble; about 15–20% of starch\n(III) Called animal starch; even more highly branched\n(IV) Water insoluble; branching through C1–C6 linkage; about 80–85% of starch","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Amylose is an unbranched, water-soluble chain of $\\alpha$-glucose units with C1–C4 links, making up 15–20% of starch: A-II.\n(B) Amylopectin is insoluble and branched through C1–C6 links, making up 80–85% of starch: B-IV.\n(C) Cellulose is a straight chain of $\\beta$-D-glucose units with C1–C4 links: C-I.\n(D) Glycogen resembles amylopectin but is more highly branched, and is called animal starch: D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Insulin is regarded as a protein even though it contains only 51 amino acid residues.\nReason (R): Polypeptides with fewer amino acids are likely to be called proteins if they ordinarily have a well-defined conformation of a protein.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Normally, a polypeptide with more than 100 residues and a molecular mass above 10,000 u is called a protein.\nHowever, NCERT notes that this distinction is not sharp.\nShorter polypeptides that have a well-defined protein conformation are also called proteins, and insulin (51 residues) is the example given.\nSo A is true, R is true, and R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Lactose, commonly called milk sugar, gives which pair of monosaccharides on complete hydrolysis?","image":"","options":["$\\alpha$-D-glucose and $\\alpha$-D-glucose","$\\beta$-D-galactose and $\\beta$-D-glucose","$\\alpha$-D-glucose and $\\beta$-D-fructose","$\\beta$-D-galactose and $\\beta$-D-fructose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, lactose is made of $\\beta$-D-galactose and $\\beta$-D-glucose units.\nThe two units are joined between C1 of galactose and C4 of glucose.\nTwo $\\alpha$-D-glucose units make up maltose, and glucose with fructose makes up sucrose. These are the tempting wrong choices.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":165,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Sucrose\n(b) Maltose\n(c) Lactose\n(d) Invert sugar\nList-II:\n(i) Two α-D-glucose units joined by a C1–C4 link\n(ii) β-D-galactose and β-D-glucose joined by a C1–C4 link\n(iii) C1 of α-D-glucose linked to C2 of β-D-fructose\n(iv) Equimolar laevorotatory mixture of glucose and fructose","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii","a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv","a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Sucrose: C1 of α-D-glucose is linked to C2 of β-D-fructose, so (iii).\n(b) Maltose: two α-D-glucose units with a C1–C4 link, so (i).\n(c) Lactose: β-D-galactose and β-D-glucose with a C1–C4 link, so (ii).\n(d) Invert sugar: the laevorotatory 1:1 glucose–fructose mixture obtained from sucrose, so (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":166,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"A patient shows hypoglycemia, general weakness and increased susceptibility to stress. Which option correctly identifies the condition, its cause and its treatment?","image":"","options":["Hypothyroidism caused by low dietary iodine; treated by using iodised salt","Hyperthyroidism caused by excess thyroxine; treated with insulin","Excess glucagon secretion from the adrenal cortex; treated with estradiol","Addison's disease caused by improper functioning of the adrenal cortex; treated with glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Hypoglycemia, weakness and increased susceptibility to stress are the features NCERT gives for Addison's disease.\nIt results when the adrenal cortex does not function properly.\nThe disease is fatal unless treated with glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids, which replace the missing adrenal cortex hormones.\nHypothyroidism is marked by lethargy and obesity, not by these symptoms.\nGlucagon is not described as an adrenal cortex hormone.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":167,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In maltose, a free aldehyde group can be generated in solution at C1 of the glucose unit that participates in the glycosidic bond only through its C4.\nStatement II: In lactose, the free aldehyde group that makes it a reducing sugar is generated at C1 of the galactose unit.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In maltose, C1 of glucose (I) is tied up in the C1–C4 link. Glucose (II) is bonded only through its C4 oxygen, so its C1 hemiacetal can open to give a free –CHO.\nStatement II is incorrect. In lactose, C1 of galactose forms the glycosidic bond with C4 of glucose. The free aldehyde therefore arises at C1 of the glucose unit, not the galactose unit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":168,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a carbohydrate whose molecular formula CANNOT be written in the form $C_x(H_2O)_y$?","image":"","options":["Maltose, $C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}$","Rhamnose, $C_6H_{12}O_5$","Fructose, $C_6H_{12}O_6$","Glucose, $C_6H_{12}O_6$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rhamnose, $C_6H_{12}O_5$, would need 6 O atoms to match 12 H atoms as $C_6(H_2O)_6$. It has only 5 O atoms, so it does not fit the formula, yet it is a carbohydrate.\nMaltose is $C_{12}(H_2O)_{11}$, which fits.\nGlucose and fructose are both $C_6(H_2O)_6$, which fits.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":169,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Glucose (dextrose) forms n-hexane on prolonged heating with HI. What does this observation establish about the structure of glucose?","image":"","options":["Glucose contains an aldehyde group","Glucose has five hydroxyl groups on different carbon atoms","All six carbon atoms of glucose are linked in a straight chain","Glucose contains a primary alcoholic group"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Prolonged heating with HI reduces glucose completely to n-hexane, $CH_3(CH_2)_4CH_3$.\nn-Hexane is an unbranched six-carbon chain, so the six carbons of glucose must also form a straight chain.\nThis test says nothing about the nature of the carbonyl group. Bromine water oxidation is what shows that the carbonyl group is an aldehyde.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":170,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the two crystalline forms of glucose is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The α-form melts at 419 K and the β-form melts at 423 K.","The β-form is obtained by crystallisation from a concentrated solution of glucose at 303 K.","The α- and β-forms differ only in the configuration of the –OH group at C-1.","Both forms are in equilibrium with the open-chain form in solution."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the α-form melts at 419 K and the β-form at 423 K.\n(2) Incorrect: crystallisation from a concentrated solution at 303 K gives the α-form. The β-form comes from a hot, saturated aqueous solution at 371 K.\n(3) Correct: the two forms are anomers and differ only at the anomeric carbon, C-1.\n(4) Correct: both cyclic forms exist in equilibrium with the open-chain structure.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-BIOMOLECULES-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Biomolecules","qno":171,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Biomolecules","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following compounds fits the general formula $C_x(H_2O)_y$ but is NOT a carbohydrate?","image":"","options":["Rhamnose, $C_6H_{12}O_5$","Acetic acid, $CH_3COOH$","Sucrose, $C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}$","Glucose, $C_6H_{12}O_6$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Acetic acid, $C_2H_4O_2$, can be written as $C_2(H_2O)_2$, so it fits the formula. It is not a polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone, so it is not a carbohydrate.\nGlucose, $C_6(H_2O)_6$, and sucrose, $C_{12}(H_2O)_{11}$, fit the formula and are carbohydrates.\nRhamnose is a carbohydrate, but it does not fit the formula.\nSo the formula alone cannot define a carbohydrate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 10 · Biomolecules","difficulty":"easy"}]});
